WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:03.600
Greetings everybody and welcome to Rollback

2
00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:06.340
our brand new YouTube only format

3
00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:08.870
where we share the best evergreen conversations

4
00:00:08.870 --> 00:00:12.030
from our pre-video catalog with all of you.

5
00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:16.630
Today, we bring you episode number 243 from August of 2016,

6
00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:19.530
where I had the great honor of sitting down

7
00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:23.800
with one of the legendary basketball coaches of all time,

8
00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:25.430
George Raveling.

9
00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:29.330
Affectionately known as just Coach, George is many things.

10
00:00:29.330 --> 00:00:32.880
He's Nike's director of international basketball,

11
00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:35.650
he was the first African American assistant coach

12
00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:38.410
of Villanova, the first African American assistant coach

13
00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:40.180
at University of Maryland,

14
00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:43.760
and seven year spent as head coach at USC

15
00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:46.820
where he became quite active and outspoken

16
00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:50.440
on social issues at the intersection of race, education,

17
00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:51.950
and athletics.

18
00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:55.150
George retired from coaching in 1994,

19
00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:57.380
but to this day is considered

20
00:00:57.380 --> 00:01:00.400
one of the most respected and revered figures

21
00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:03.550
in not just basketball but in all of sport.

22
00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:05.080
He is a national treasurer,

23
00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:07.760
just an absolute gem of a human being,

24
00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:12.510
a man full of life experiences and incredible stories.

25
00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:16.940
Not the least of which is how on August 28th, 1963,

26
00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:18.640
George came to be standing alongside

27
00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:21.350
Dr. Martin Luther King Jr. at the podium

28
00:01:21.350 --> 00:01:23.580
on the steps of the Lincoln Memorial

29
00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:26.490
during the march on Washington as Dr. King delivered

30
00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:29.510
his historic, "I Have a Dream" speech.

31
00:01:29.510 --> 00:01:32.210
There is so much more to this extraordinary story,

32
00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:34.040
but I don't wanna spoil anything for you

33
00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:38.280
so please enjoy this Rollback to a mind blowing episode

34
00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:40.113
with Coach George Raveling.

35
00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:45.610
So we were introduced to each other by our mutual friend,

36
00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:47.890
Ryan Holiday who I'm a big fan of

37
00:01:47.890 --> 00:01:50.080
I know you are as well.
Yeah, absolutely.

38
00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:52.000
I tell him I'm president of his fan club.

39
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:53.900
Yeah, he's got quite a fan club

40
00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:57.460
and he speaks very, very highly of you.

41
00:01:57.460 --> 00:01:59.480
He was insistent that we get together

42
00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:01.280
and any friend of Ryan's is a friend of mine

43
00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:02.830
so it's a pleasure and an honor

44
00:02:03.735 --> 00:02:05.650
to be able to spend a little time with you today.

45
00:02:05.650 --> 00:02:07.460
Well, I echo the same sentiments for you,

46
00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:10.450
it's an honor for me to be on your podcast.

47
00:02:10.450 --> 00:02:11.370
I appreciate that.

48
00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:12.500
And just to set the stage,

49
00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:14.650
we're sitting in this beautiful library,

50
00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:17.350
that is wall to wall books, Florida ceiling.

51
00:02:17.350 --> 00:02:19.870
Have you read all of these books?

52
00:02:19.870 --> 00:02:20.703
No, no.

53
00:02:20.703 --> 00:02:21.627
The answer is no, but I've read--

54
00:02:21.627 --> 00:02:24.100
People send you a lot of books.

55
00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:25.480
Well, I buy a lot of books,

56
00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:26.980
but people send them to me too.

57
00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:31.980
But I'm well fortified if the bookstores go out of business,

58
00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:35.470
I'll have enough books in here

59
00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:36.940
to last me for the rest of my--

60
00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:37.773
Yeah, I think you're good.

61
00:02:37.773 --> 00:02:38.606
My lifetime.

62
00:02:38.606 --> 00:02:39.439
So I'll be...

63
00:02:39.439 --> 00:02:41.900
I'm well stocked and I keep buying them.

64
00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:43.860
Well, you should, right?

65
00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:46.550
And I think reading is a big part of your story,

66
00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:48.680
we're gonna get into the background and all of that,

67
00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:51.480
but I know that reading is very important to you

68
00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:54.397
and you have your Reading with Rav, right?

69
00:02:54.397 --> 00:02:55.519
Is that what it's called, that goes way back?

70
00:02:55.519 --> 00:02:56.352
Reading with Raveling.

71
00:02:56.352 --> 00:02:58.270
Yes, I started that back in the '70s.

72
00:02:58.270 --> 00:02:59.680
Yeah, let's talk about that a little bit,

73
00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:01.040
the importance of reading to you

74
00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:03.660
and kind of your advocacy around that.

75
00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:07.360
Well, I think at the time

76
00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:11.260
we had not become a technological society

77
00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:13.440
in the sense that we are today,

78
00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:16.170
but I think early on I realized

79
00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:18.470
the unique value of information.

80
00:03:18.470 --> 00:03:20.590
I used to tell my players that

81
00:03:20.590 --> 00:03:23.520
perhaps the two most important commodities in the world

82
00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:24.930
were information and money.

83
00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:27.970
If you've got money, you can buy information.

84
00:03:27.970 --> 00:03:31.380
If you've got information, you can get plenty of money.

85
00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:36.380
And little did I know that we'd evolve into a culture

86
00:03:36.910 --> 00:03:38.240
that we presently live in

87
00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:41.630
where it's so dominated by information

88
00:03:41.630 --> 00:03:46.050
and so much information is readily available to us

89
00:03:46.050 --> 00:03:48.760
that you can...

90
00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:51.660
If a person wanted to self-educate themself,

91
00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:54.390
all they have to do is get a smart phone

92
00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:57.420
and use Google servers.

93
00:03:57.420 --> 00:04:00.620
About anything you can imagine, if you Google it,

94
00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:04.200
you'll get anywhere from 10 to 20 articles on it

95
00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:05.390
and so it's...

96
00:04:05.390 --> 00:04:09.440
I think that it's comforting for me to know

97
00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:14.160
that I can continue to educate myself at 79

98
00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:17.170
and take myself from where I am

99
00:04:17.170 --> 00:04:19.970
to perhaps where I should be intellectually

100
00:04:19.970 --> 00:04:23.190
and from a knowledge gathering standpoint.

101
00:04:23.190 --> 00:04:24.023
Yeah

102
00:04:24.023 --> 00:04:26.820
I mean, I think technology is really shifting how we learn

103
00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:28.150
and how we educate ourself.

104
00:04:28.150 --> 00:04:31.100
It's democratizing it in a large way

105
00:04:31.100 --> 00:04:33.740
and it's gonna be interesting to see how that plays out

106
00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:35.470
over the next 10 or 20 years

107
00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:37.740
and the impact that that has on

108
00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:40.650
the value of the college experience and college education

109
00:04:40.650 --> 00:04:43.230
when you can kind of take those matters into your own hands,

110
00:04:43.230 --> 00:04:47.020
irregardless of your socioeconomic situation.

111
00:04:47.020 --> 00:04:48.610
And that's a great point.

112
00:04:48.610 --> 00:04:53.340
It really renders the excuse that we have poor teachers

113
00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:54.380
or poor schools.

114
00:04:54.380 --> 00:04:56.430
It really marginalizes that (indistinct),

115
00:04:57.610 --> 00:05:01.480
because if you have poor books or poor teachers

116
00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:04.890
teach yourself and all you have to have is a smartphone

117
00:05:04.890 --> 00:05:06.050
to be able to do it.

118
00:05:06.050 --> 00:05:11.050
But to race back to the Reading with Raveling,

119
00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:14.000
I started in the '70s and I felt that

120
00:05:14.930 --> 00:05:19.230
a lot of the young people that I came in contact with

121
00:05:20.710 --> 00:05:25.060
were not avid readers and when I ask them why they said

122
00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:26.830
that it's boring.

123
00:05:26.830 --> 00:05:30.650
And so I tried to be an active listener

124
00:05:30.650 --> 00:05:32.690
and they didn't say they didn't like to read

125
00:05:32.690 --> 00:05:33.930
they just said it was boring.

126
00:05:33.930 --> 00:05:36.620
So I started to think to myself,

127
00:05:36.620 --> 00:05:41.023
if I can present to them the right topics,

128
00:05:42.010 --> 00:05:43.730
maybe I can get them hooked on reading.

129
00:05:43.730 --> 00:05:46.870
So if a kid like music, I give him books about music.

130
00:05:46.870 --> 00:05:49.570
If he like sports, I give him books about sports.

131
00:05:49.570 --> 00:05:52.360
I tried to make his ascension

132
00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:55.490
from where he was to where he could be reading wise,

133
00:05:55.490 --> 00:06:00.490
easy for him and not to give them the most complex books.

134
00:06:00.730 --> 00:06:03.070
And the other avenue that I discovered

135
00:06:03.070 --> 00:06:07.470
is particularly for younger people is to read to them.

136
00:06:07.470 --> 00:06:12.470
Because there's always a child like characteristic

137
00:06:13.050 --> 00:06:14.730
in all of us, even at 79,

138
00:06:14.730 --> 00:06:18.220
I still enjoy hearing nice, intriguing stories.

139
00:06:18.220 --> 00:06:21.463
And so I think when you read to a child,

140
00:06:23.495 --> 00:06:27.470
it stimulates their mind, it takes them on a mental journey,

141
00:06:27.470 --> 00:06:29.430
it heightens their curiosity.

142
00:06:29.430 --> 00:06:34.430
And so what I tried to do was to give books to young people

143
00:06:34.780 --> 00:06:37.120
and not reward them for reading them.

144
00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:40.000
When they'd bring them back, I'd give them a little test

145
00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:42.780
and I'd reward them for reading.

146
00:06:42.780 --> 00:06:47.780
And over the years, I began to realize that I'd struck upon

147
00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:51.480
something from an ethnic standpoint and that is that,

148
00:06:51.480 --> 00:06:55.160
back in the days of slavery, the plantation owners

149
00:06:55.160 --> 00:06:57.380
used to hide their money in the books

150
00:06:57.380 --> 00:06:59.970
and put the books up on the shelves

151
00:06:59.970 --> 00:07:03.170
because they never worried about the slaves stealing

152
00:07:03.170 --> 00:07:04.993
the books or the money because they couldn't read.

153
00:07:04.993 --> 00:07:06.264
That's so terrible

154
00:07:06.264 --> 00:07:07.590
when you think about that.
And so what...

155
00:07:07.590 --> 00:07:10.130
How I translated that was

156
00:07:10.130 --> 00:07:13.600
that as long as someone can control your mind,

157
00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:15.530
they can control your body.

158
00:07:15.530 --> 00:07:20.530
And so if we don't allow people to possess our minds,

159
00:07:22.650 --> 00:07:24.860
then they cannot possess our bodies

160
00:07:24.860 --> 00:07:29.860
and so how do we achieve that by filling our head

161
00:07:30.170 --> 00:07:33.290
with as much information, as much knowledge,

162
00:07:33.290 --> 00:07:37.930
as much material as it possibly can.

163
00:07:37.930 --> 00:07:41.210
And along the way either by intended

164
00:07:42.790 --> 00:07:45.910
or unintended consequence is that

165
00:07:45.910 --> 00:07:48.860
it stimulates your critical and analytical thinking.

166
00:07:48.860 --> 00:07:50.440
It's just a byproduct of it.

167
00:07:50.440 --> 00:07:53.273
At some point, if you read enough,

168
00:07:54.456 --> 00:07:57.507
it's gonna cause you to think different, to act different,

169
00:07:57.507 --> 00:07:59.480
and to be different.

170
00:07:59.480 --> 00:08:01.180
But it requires a certain level

171
00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:04.090
of self discipline and willingness.

172
00:08:04.090 --> 00:08:05.950
And it reminds me of a speech that you gave

173
00:08:05.950 --> 00:08:09.000
that I listened to the other day I think it's from 1977,

174
00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:12.660
it was on your podcast where the basic theme of it was,

175
00:08:12.660 --> 00:08:14.067
it's up to you.

176
00:08:14.067 --> 00:08:15.640
(indistinct) is to be it's up to me.

177
00:08:15.640 --> 00:08:18.040
And that was a talk that I did

178
00:08:18.040 --> 00:08:22.430
to kids at our by basketball camp back in the '70s.

179
00:08:22.430 --> 00:08:25.440
And as cliche says it sounds,

180
00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:28.023
I think it's this powerful statement,

181
00:08:29.700 --> 00:08:32.090
research would substantiate it.

182
00:08:32.090 --> 00:08:35.120
And that is that at the end of the day,

183
00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:37.870
nobody's gonna row our boat for us but ourselves.

184
00:08:37.870 --> 00:08:39.410
We either...

185
00:08:39.410 --> 00:08:43.850
Our lives are gonna be lived in a relatively orderly manner

186
00:08:43.850 --> 00:08:45.550
and so either our hands

187
00:08:45.550 --> 00:08:47.750
are gonna be on the steering wheel of our life

188
00:08:47.750 --> 00:08:50.660
or someone else's hands are gonna be on the steering wheel

189
00:08:50.660 --> 00:08:52.010
of our lives.

190
00:08:52.010 --> 00:08:55.074
And I prefer that they...

191
00:08:55.074 --> 00:09:00.074
That I govern my life and the way I think and I've been...

192
00:09:02.440 --> 00:09:05.000
Actually, you're probably the first one

193
00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:07.010
that I've talked to publicly about this,

194
00:09:07.010 --> 00:09:10.080
but I've been working on this theory

195
00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:13.210
that I'm gonna continue to work on over the year

196
00:09:13.210 --> 00:09:15.940
until I get it really well defined,

197
00:09:15.940 --> 00:09:18.880
but I call it environmental control.

198
00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:21.400
And the theory behind this is

199
00:09:21.400 --> 00:09:25.900
if you can control the environment in which you live,

200
00:09:25.900 --> 00:09:30.570
then you can create the necessary environment

201
00:09:32.300 --> 00:09:36.250
to live a peaceful, productive life, to be happy,

202
00:09:36.250 --> 00:09:39.880
if in fact happiness is something that's important to you.

203
00:09:39.880 --> 00:09:40.900
And so

204
00:09:44.140 --> 00:09:46.330
the idea is to create a community

205
00:09:46.330 --> 00:09:49.233
within this universal community.

206
00:09:50.996 --> 00:09:53.290
And I can control the community that I live in.

207
00:09:53.290 --> 00:09:55.610
I can control the people I associate with,

208
00:09:55.610 --> 00:10:00.390
the books I read, my values, what I watch on television,

209
00:10:00.390 --> 00:10:01.453
how I think.

210
00:10:02.310 --> 00:10:04.750
We have a lot more control over our lives

211
00:10:04.750 --> 00:10:06.400
than we realize that we do.

212
00:10:06.400 --> 00:10:10.490
I just don't think that our thinking

213
00:10:10.490 --> 00:10:14.600
is maybe as sophisticated as it could be or would be

214
00:10:14.600 --> 00:10:16.210
if we decided...

215
00:10:16.210 --> 00:10:21.210
If everyone decided once and for all that,

216
00:10:21.290 --> 00:10:23.320
I'm gonna be the captain of my ship,

217
00:10:23.320 --> 00:10:25.823
I'm gonna control my destiny,

218
00:10:25.823 --> 00:10:30.173
I'm gonna create a path to success for me.

219
00:10:31.667 --> 00:10:33.470
And if they started to control

220
00:10:33.470 --> 00:10:37.363
the people that they choose to be around,

221
00:10:38.420 --> 00:10:41.520
what they listen to, what they watch, their values.

222
00:10:41.520 --> 00:10:43.830
I think it's very easy to do

223
00:10:43.830 --> 00:10:48.620
and I didn't actually think about this in a whole lot

224
00:10:48.620 --> 00:10:50.390
until the last four or five years

225
00:10:50.390 --> 00:10:54.100
but I really have put that into play.

226
00:10:54.100 --> 00:10:59.100
I'm very watchful of what I eat,

227
00:11:00.490 --> 00:11:03.070
where I go, what I do,

228
00:11:03.070 --> 00:11:06.310
and I try to create an environment in which

229
00:11:06.310 --> 00:11:09.023
I feel that I can exist.

230
00:11:11.930 --> 00:11:14.440
and feel like I'm making progress.

231
00:11:14.440 --> 00:11:19.440
I'm not a person that believes in happiness.

232
00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:22.200
I think to me happiness is

233
00:11:23.570 --> 00:11:27.930
if you spend your whole life chasing happiness,

234
00:11:27.930 --> 00:11:30.130
I think you're gonna be chasing a (indistinct)

235
00:11:30.130 --> 00:11:32.910
because the bar's always gonna be moving.

236
00:11:32.910 --> 00:11:37.150
And most times happiness is validated by someone else.

237
00:11:39.620 --> 00:11:41.620
And so to me,

238
00:11:41.620 --> 00:11:45.330
rather than go through all the anxiety of being happy,

239
00:11:45.330 --> 00:11:46.163
I never...

240
00:11:47.050 --> 00:11:49.080
This is the first time actually in a long time

241
00:11:49.080 --> 00:11:50.720
that I even talked about happiness,

242
00:11:50.720 --> 00:11:55.720
because I try to eradicate that word from my vocabulary.

243
00:11:56.010 --> 00:11:57.710
The idea of happiness

244
00:11:57.710 --> 00:11:59.090
has some sort of destination.

245
00:11:59.090 --> 00:12:00.870
Yes.
But I would presume

246
00:12:00.870 --> 00:12:04.090
that purpose and engagement,

247
00:12:04.090 --> 00:12:06.122
sort of supplants the idea of happiness.

248
00:12:06.122 --> 00:12:06.955
Yes.

249
00:12:06.955 --> 00:12:08.090
In terms of being a contented

250
00:12:08.090 --> 00:12:09.210
and directed individual.

251
00:12:09.210 --> 00:12:10.710
I'd be more...

252
00:12:10.710 --> 00:12:13.440
You picked a perfect word as it relates to me.

253
00:12:13.440 --> 00:12:17.440
I'm more interested in being contented than I am being happy

254
00:12:17.440 --> 00:12:18.273
because it...

255
00:12:18.273 --> 00:12:22.860
And then I realize that these are momentary existence,

256
00:12:22.860 --> 00:12:27.150
but you're always reaching and you get to a point

257
00:12:27.150 --> 00:12:29.230
that you live and manifest,

258
00:12:29.230 --> 00:12:32.030
something I learned at Nike when working there

259
00:12:32.030 --> 00:12:35.143
and their mantra is there is no finish line,

260
00:12:36.740 --> 00:12:41.650
that you're constantly asking yourself what's next.

261
00:12:41.650 --> 00:12:44.770
And I remember Phil Knight saying to me one time,

262
00:12:44.770 --> 00:12:48.500
the minute you think you've won, you're starting to lose.

263
00:12:48.500 --> 00:12:49.640
Yeah, nothing is static.

264
00:12:49.640 --> 00:12:50.473
Yes.

265
00:12:50.473 --> 00:12:52.130
And in between the lines

266
00:12:52.130 --> 00:12:53.990
what I'm hearing you speak to

267
00:12:53.990 --> 00:12:57.047
is this idea of self determinism, right?

268
00:12:58.492 --> 00:12:59.325
Yes.
The idea that

269
00:12:59.325 --> 00:13:00.630
you can control your environment,

270
00:13:00.630 --> 00:13:03.210
the adage that you're the average of the five people

271
00:13:03.210 --> 00:13:05.700
you spend the most time with and the extent to which

272
00:13:05.700 --> 00:13:08.370
we can control some of these factors.

273
00:13:08.370 --> 00:13:11.610
And that is an idea that I believe in wholeheartedly,

274
00:13:11.610 --> 00:13:16.130
but it also butts up against this kind of victim narrative

275
00:13:16.130 --> 00:13:18.370
that seems to populate the news

276
00:13:18.370 --> 00:13:20.710
and our culture at the moment.

277
00:13:20.710 --> 00:13:22.980
The idea that everybody is a victim

278
00:13:22.980 --> 00:13:25.040
and there's a lot of finger pointing

279
00:13:25.040 --> 00:13:28.830
and blaming other people for the circumstances of our lives.

280
00:13:28.830 --> 00:13:32.960
And on some level you're a rebuttal to that I would say.

281
00:13:32.960 --> 00:13:33.793
Is that it?

282
00:13:33.793 --> 00:13:37.080
Well, as it relates to me

283
00:13:37.938 --> 00:13:41.220
in the minute you start to try to reach out

284
00:13:41.220 --> 00:13:45.440
into these other communities and connect with them

285
00:13:45.440 --> 00:13:50.440
even if it's by way of conversation or just mentally,

286
00:13:50.560 --> 00:13:52.980
I think that you now run the risk

287
00:13:52.980 --> 00:13:55.780
that they're gonna try to validate who you are,

288
00:13:55.780 --> 00:13:57.550
what you say, or what you do.

289
00:13:57.550 --> 00:14:00.050
And I'm really...

290
00:14:00.050 --> 00:14:03.660
I think I'm perfectly capable of validating myself.

291
00:14:03.660 --> 00:14:07.140
I don't have to live by someone else's norms

292
00:14:07.140 --> 00:14:09.303
or standards for a validation.

293
00:14:10.795 --> 00:14:14.140
I used to say in a talk that I did that,

294
00:14:14.140 --> 00:14:16.120
the hardest battle that person

295
00:14:16.120 --> 00:14:19.010
has to fight in their lifetime is to live in a world

296
00:14:19.010 --> 00:14:20.910
where every single day someone's trying

297
00:14:20.910 --> 00:14:23.160
to make you be someone you don't wanna be.

298
00:14:23.160 --> 00:14:24.930
I think I know who I wanna be,

299
00:14:24.930 --> 00:14:27.550
I don't need someone to try to make me be

300
00:14:27.550 --> 00:14:30.870
who they want me to be or to live by their values.

301
00:14:30.870 --> 00:14:33.540
In that I respect their values,

302
00:14:33.540 --> 00:14:35.060
I respect their way of life,

303
00:14:35.060 --> 00:14:38.630
and I hope that they would do the same for me.

304
00:14:38.630 --> 00:14:40.300
Well, let's track it back,

305
00:14:40.300 --> 00:14:43.430
the superhero origin story so to speak.

306
00:14:43.430 --> 00:14:45.900
You lived through some very interesting times,

307
00:14:45.900 --> 00:14:48.850
some pivotal times in our history

308
00:14:48.850 --> 00:14:51.740
and you've played a significant role not just in sport

309
00:14:51.740 --> 00:14:54.790
but in the culture at large through your involvement

310
00:14:54.790 --> 00:14:57.100
in the civil rights movement, et cetera.

311
00:14:57.100 --> 00:14:59.800
But where does it all begin?

312
00:14:59.800 --> 00:15:01.500
I know it begins in Washington, DC

313
00:15:01.500 --> 00:15:05.190
and had sort of an interesting family situation

314
00:15:05.190 --> 00:15:06.200
when you were a kid.

315
00:15:06.200 --> 00:15:07.450
It actually...

316
00:15:09.360 --> 00:15:13.380
As I look quickly, try to reflect on it

317
00:15:13.380 --> 00:15:16.440
and you probably touched upon

318
00:15:17.440 --> 00:15:21.990
a real important item in my life

319
00:15:21.990 --> 00:15:23.700
and that was the Washington, DC.

320
00:15:23.700 --> 00:15:25.730
I was born in Washington, DC,

321
00:15:25.730 --> 00:15:29.640
then you fast forward to the march on Washington,

322
00:15:29.640 --> 00:15:31.190
which is in Washington, DC.

323
00:15:31.190 --> 00:15:35.640
But I grew up in Washington, DC during the depression

324
00:15:35.640 --> 00:15:39.250
when things were rationed, sugar was rationed,

325
00:15:39.250 --> 00:15:41.053
flour and so forth.

326
00:15:43.294 --> 00:15:47.900
And so when I was nine years old, my dad died

327
00:15:47.900 --> 00:15:51.430
and when I was 13 my mom had a nervous breakdown

328
00:15:51.430 --> 00:15:55.560
and she was institutionalized at St. Elizabeths Hospital

329
00:15:56.960 --> 00:16:00.610
in Washington, DC for most of the rest of her life.

330
00:16:00.610 --> 00:16:05.610
And so now what happens is, by and large, I'm an orphan.

331
00:16:08.787 --> 00:16:11.600
And so what do you do with George?

332
00:16:11.600 --> 00:16:15.850
And so my grandmother was really

333
00:16:15.850 --> 00:16:17.963
the matriarch of our family.

334
00:16:18.810 --> 00:16:21.060
I always envisioned her like the Pope.

335
00:16:21.060 --> 00:16:24.080
She was infallible, whatever she said went.

336
00:16:24.080 --> 00:16:26.930
And so my grandma worked for this white family

337
00:16:26.930 --> 00:16:30.450
in Georgetown, and she had mentioned to them

338
00:16:30.450 --> 00:16:35.240
about the circumstances and so the lady of the house said,

339
00:16:35.240 --> 00:16:38.630
well, I think that her daughter's name was Rachel.

340
00:16:38.630 --> 00:16:41.430
She said, well, Rachel works for the Catholic charities

341
00:16:41.430 --> 00:16:43.360
she might be able to help you.

342
00:16:43.360 --> 00:16:45.110
And lo and behold, she did.

343
00:16:45.110 --> 00:16:48.680
She was able to get me in a Catholic boarding school

344
00:16:48.680 --> 00:16:51.020
in upstate Pennsylvania, in a place called

345
00:16:51.020 --> 00:16:52.950
Hoover Heights, Pennsylvania.

346
00:16:52.950 --> 00:16:56.020
Geographically, it falls between Scranton

347
00:16:56.020 --> 00:16:57.730
and Piston, Pennsylvania

348
00:16:57.730 --> 00:17:01.200
up in the Northeastern part of the state.

349
00:17:01.200 --> 00:17:06.200
And so I finished the rest of my education there.

350
00:17:07.105 --> 00:17:11.090
I finished upgrade school, what we would call junior high

351
00:17:11.090 --> 00:17:12.693
and then I went to high school.

352
00:17:13.690 --> 00:17:16.320
And growing up without a father and a mother

353
00:17:16.320 --> 00:17:18.050
and sort of being raised by your grandmother

354
00:17:18.050 --> 00:17:19.520
and bouncing around a little bit,

355
00:17:19.520 --> 00:17:23.710
I would imagine that's where the seeds of self determinism

356
00:17:23.710 --> 00:17:26.230
probably were planted originally, right?

357
00:17:26.230 --> 00:17:28.240
This idea that you're gonna have to be responsible

358
00:17:28.240 --> 00:17:31.070
for yourself and having to shoulder that mantle

359
00:17:31.070 --> 00:17:32.170
at a very young age.

360
00:17:32.170 --> 00:17:33.800
Yeah, I didn't have the luxury

361
00:17:33.800 --> 00:17:38.610
that comes with a family and a mom and dad in the sense

362
00:17:38.610 --> 00:17:40.340
that I didn't have someone there

363
00:17:40.340 --> 00:17:42.180
that was scripting out my day every...

364
00:17:42.180 --> 00:17:45.390
Well I did, but it was a lot different because

365
00:17:46.430 --> 00:17:48.800
when you're in a family,

366
00:17:48.800 --> 00:17:50.810
there might only be four or five of you

367
00:17:50.810 --> 00:17:52.520
but at the school where I was

368
00:17:52.520 --> 00:17:54.520
there were over 200 young men there

369
00:17:56.215 --> 00:18:01.215
and basically we were in a very disciplined environment

370
00:18:02.290 --> 00:18:06.680
in order to sort of pay your way,

371
00:18:06.680 --> 00:18:08.650
you had to do chores at the school.

372
00:18:08.650 --> 00:18:10.920
So the time that I was there I did everything

373
00:18:10.920 --> 00:18:14.320
from clean out chicken coops, to picking eggs, baling hay,

374
00:18:14.320 --> 00:18:15.480
working in the bakery,

375
00:18:15.480 --> 00:18:18.943
scrubbing the chapel floors on my hands and knees,

376
00:18:20.040 --> 00:18:22.230
working in the laundry.

377
00:18:22.230 --> 00:18:27.230
By the time that I graduated from there, I was fully trained

378
00:18:29.880 --> 00:18:31.980
to live the rest of my life

379
00:18:31.980 --> 00:18:34.580
and be able to take care of myself.

380
00:18:34.580 --> 00:18:37.520
Even to this day I get up every morning

381
00:18:37.520 --> 00:18:41.190
and I iron my pants and my shirt before I go off to work

382
00:18:41.190 --> 00:18:43.430
and I'm 79 years old.

383
00:18:43.430 --> 00:18:44.263
Yeah, you make your bed

384
00:18:44.263 --> 00:18:46.130
you bounce a nickel off it.

385
00:18:46.130 --> 00:18:49.590
Well, it was funny when I was in the army

386
00:18:49.590 --> 00:18:51.640
and a lot of people get surprised

387
00:18:51.640 --> 00:18:52.930
when I tell them I was in the army

388
00:18:52.930 --> 00:18:56.010
but (indistinct) mandatory draft when I was there.

389
00:18:56.010 --> 00:18:58.330
And so in basic training,

390
00:18:58.330 --> 00:19:02.780
I did my basic training in Fort Knox, Kentucky.

391
00:19:02.780 --> 00:19:06.110
And so you had to make your bed every morning

392
00:19:06.110 --> 00:19:09.960
and stand for inspection and I was absolutely...

393
00:19:09.960 --> 00:19:12.250
I just assumed everybody knew how to make a bed

394
00:19:12.250 --> 00:19:13.430
and guys were paying...

395
00:19:13.430 --> 00:19:15.080
Guys were willing to pay me 50 cents

396
00:19:15.080 --> 00:19:18.060
to make their bed in the morning and that and so it...

397
00:19:19.010 --> 00:19:23.080
But all those little fundamental lessons

398
00:19:23.920 --> 00:19:26.620
that I learned in school they all came back

399
00:19:29.356 --> 00:19:30.690
to help me along the way.

400
00:19:30.690 --> 00:19:34.380
I mean, I even learned how to drive a tractor

401
00:19:34.380 --> 00:19:35.930
over the course of the time there.

402
00:19:35.930 --> 00:19:40.330
So what I did is I had a broad landscape

403
00:19:40.330 --> 00:19:43.750
of learning opportunities outside the classroom.

404
00:19:43.750 --> 00:19:46.680
It was one thing to be able to get tremendous

405
00:19:47.610 --> 00:19:49.930
formal education in the classroom

406
00:19:49.930 --> 00:19:53.510
but when you can couple that with life lessons

407
00:19:53.510 --> 00:19:56.310
outside of the classroom,

408
00:19:56.310 --> 00:19:59.560
that's a unique opportunity to grow and learn.

409
00:19:59.560 --> 00:20:00.950
And that's a theme that I think

410
00:20:00.950 --> 00:20:04.000
kind of over arches your coaching career, right?

411
00:20:04.000 --> 00:20:05.720
The idea that these aren't just athletes,

412
00:20:05.720 --> 00:20:09.040
it's not just about what happens at practice

413
00:20:09.040 --> 00:20:09.873
or during the game,

414
00:20:09.873 --> 00:20:11.860
but it's about shaping the hearts and the lines

415
00:20:11.860 --> 00:20:13.080
of these young men.

416
00:20:13.080 --> 00:20:17.930
Well, for me, what I concluded later in life

417
00:20:17.930 --> 00:20:21.120
is that for me, athletics were the vehicle

418
00:20:21.120 --> 00:20:23.600
that transported me along in life

419
00:20:24.780 --> 00:20:28.760
and dropped me off at the right bus stops along the way

420
00:20:28.760 --> 00:20:32.160
and I'd stop for a while and learn and get back on.

421
00:20:32.160 --> 00:20:34.350
Athletics would take me a little further

422
00:20:34.350 --> 00:20:36.490
I would've never been able to go to college

423
00:20:36.490 --> 00:20:39.370
if it wasn't for basketball.

424
00:20:39.370 --> 00:20:42.420
And I tell you an interesting story.

425
00:20:42.420 --> 00:20:46.150
When I was a senior in high school,

426
00:20:46.150 --> 00:20:49.470
I was a second leading scorer in the state of Pennsylvania

427
00:20:49.470 --> 00:20:52.450
and so school started recruiting me.

428
00:20:52.450 --> 00:20:54.730
So one time we were playing this team

429
00:20:54.730 --> 00:20:57.760
St. Rose of Carbondale, Pennsylvania

430
00:20:57.760 --> 00:21:00.180
and when I came out after the game,

431
00:21:00.180 --> 00:21:02.600
a gentleman came up to me and he introduced himself.

432
00:21:02.600 --> 00:21:04.257
He says, "Hi, my name's Jack Ramsey.

433
00:21:04.257 --> 00:21:06.557
"I'm the coach at St. Joe's College

434
00:21:06.557 --> 00:21:09.297
"and we were very interested in you."

435
00:21:09.297 --> 00:21:11.080
And so I just listened.

436
00:21:11.080 --> 00:21:14.940
I was always taught when grownups talk

437
00:21:14.940 --> 00:21:17.240
you keep your mouth shut and you listen.

438
00:21:17.240 --> 00:21:18.990
And so he said...

439
00:21:18.990 --> 00:21:21.960
And he went on and told me a little bit about St. Joe's

440
00:21:21.960 --> 00:21:24.400
and where it was located in Philadelphia and so forth.

441
00:21:24.400 --> 00:21:27.000
And then he said, "We're gonna offer you a scholarship."

442
00:21:27.000 --> 00:21:30.100
And so he said, "We'll send something to you in writing."

443
00:21:30.100 --> 00:21:31.390
And I thanked him in that

444
00:21:31.390 --> 00:21:33.970
and so when we got on the school bus to go back,

445
00:21:33.970 --> 00:21:35.527
my coach said to me, he said,

446
00:21:35.527 --> 00:21:37.367
"Who was that gentleman you were talking to?"

447
00:21:37.367 --> 00:21:42.107
And I told him what had happened and I said to him, I said,

448
00:21:42.107 --> 00:21:44.880
"Coach, let me ask you a question, what's a scholarship?"

449
00:21:44.880 --> 00:21:46.347
I didn't even know what a scholarship was.

450
00:21:46.347 --> 00:21:47.510
Oh man.

451
00:21:47.510 --> 00:21:51.240
And it was such so foreign to me

452
00:21:51.240 --> 00:21:55.493
and to my family that when later,

453
00:21:56.370 --> 00:21:59.670
when it was just about time to graduate,

454
00:21:59.670 --> 00:22:01.250
my grandma didn't...

455
00:22:01.250 --> 00:22:03.430
For some reason I hadn't told her that

456
00:22:03.430 --> 00:22:05.610
about the scholarship and so she was asking me,

457
00:22:05.610 --> 00:22:07.610
well, what are you gonna do?

458
00:22:07.610 --> 00:22:09.260
So I say, "Well, I'm gonna go to college."

459
00:22:09.260 --> 00:22:12.427
And she said, "Well, how are you gonna go to college?

460
00:22:12.427 --> 00:22:13.520
"Who's gonna pay for it?"

461
00:22:13.520 --> 00:22:15.640
And so I said, "Oh, I'm getting a scholarship."

462
00:22:15.640 --> 00:22:17.870
And so she didn't know what the scholarship was

463
00:22:17.870 --> 00:22:22.250
so I explain to her and so it seemed like for two minutes

464
00:22:22.250 --> 00:22:24.220
but it was probably only 10 seconds,

465
00:22:24.220 --> 00:22:26.290
but she didn't say anything.

466
00:22:26.290 --> 00:22:30.040
So I said, "Well, grandma, you look like you're puzzled."

467
00:22:30.040 --> 00:22:31.290
And she said, "Well, I am."

468
00:22:31.290 --> 00:22:32.140
And I said, "Why?"

469
00:22:32.140 --> 00:22:34.147
She said, "Well, 'cause I thought

470
00:22:34.147 --> 00:22:35.867
"I raised you better than that."

471
00:22:35.867 --> 00:22:38.627
And I said, "Well, you did...

472
00:22:38.627 --> 00:22:41.560
"I think you've done a great job in raising me."

473
00:22:41.560 --> 00:22:43.737
And she says, "But I'm disappointed in myself

474
00:22:43.737 --> 00:22:47.477
"because I can't believe that you're naive enough to think

475
00:22:47.477 --> 00:22:50.107
"that some white people are gonna pay for you

476
00:22:50.107 --> 00:22:52.287
"to go to college just so you can play basketball.

477
00:22:52.287 --> 00:22:55.560
"It makes no sense, (indistinct) they're tricking you."

478
00:22:55.560 --> 00:22:56.987
She says, "It doesn't work."

479
00:22:56.987 --> 00:23:00.120
And you know, now it's during the hike of segregation

480
00:23:00.120 --> 00:23:03.520
and that she couldn't comprehend

481
00:23:03.520 --> 00:23:06.380
that white, black, blue that anybody's gonna

482
00:23:06.380 --> 00:23:10.420
pay for your education to play basketball.

483
00:23:10.420 --> 00:23:11.540
Yeah, times have changed.

484
00:23:11.540 --> 00:23:14.750
But what was the kind of landscape

485
00:23:14.750 --> 00:23:17.660
of African American athletes when you were...

486
00:23:17.660 --> 00:23:20.510
First of all, when you were in boarding school, I mean,

487
00:23:20.510 --> 00:23:23.610
you must have been one of the few African American students

488
00:23:23.610 --> 00:23:25.687
in your school to begin with, right?

489
00:23:25.687 --> 00:23:26.520
And then what about--

490
00:23:26.520 --> 00:23:27.430
With about probably about 5%

491
00:23:27.430 --> 00:23:30.970
of the student body were at that time

492
00:23:30.970 --> 00:23:33.230
they called us either colored or negros.

493
00:23:33.230 --> 00:23:35.110
And so there were about

494
00:23:36.540 --> 00:23:40.760
probably I'd say maybe 5% of the student body

495
00:23:40.760 --> 00:23:44.430
were what we would call today, African Americans

496
00:23:44.430 --> 00:23:47.210
and that and obviously we all participate in sports.

497
00:23:47.210 --> 00:23:52.210
One thing about the sports from an unusual perspective was

498
00:23:54.540 --> 00:23:56.570
it didn't take us long to figure out

499
00:23:56.570 --> 00:23:58.280
you wanted to participate in sports

500
00:23:58.280 --> 00:24:00.490
because you got to go off for campus

501
00:24:01.340 --> 00:24:04.130
and you got certain privileges.

502
00:24:04.130 --> 00:24:06.460
And so maybe instead of having

503
00:24:06.460 --> 00:24:09.600
to go and work in the bakery in the afternoon,

504
00:24:09.600 --> 00:24:11.930
I got to go to basketball practice or I got...

505
00:24:11.930 --> 00:24:13.950
So I played every sport.

506
00:24:13.950 --> 00:24:18.950
I played baseball, basketball, football, and boxing.

507
00:24:20.100 --> 00:24:21.980
You were at Golden Gloves in boxing, right?

508
00:24:21.980 --> 00:24:23.330
Yeah, we had a...

509
00:24:23.330 --> 00:24:27.940
They had a golden glove boxing team and so I went out for it

510
00:24:27.940 --> 00:24:30.030
and whatever sport was gonna

511
00:24:30.030 --> 00:24:33.020
get me some special privileges and get me off campus

512
00:24:33.020 --> 00:24:33.853
I was for it.

513
00:24:33.853 --> 00:24:35.080
But basketball was the thing.

514
00:24:35.080 --> 00:24:35.920
Yes.

515
00:24:35.920 --> 00:24:38.510
And then I didn't actually start playing

516
00:24:38.510 --> 00:24:41.790
organized basketball until I was a freshman in high school.

517
00:24:41.790 --> 00:24:44.620
And I found out later my godfather was a priest

518
00:24:44.620 --> 00:24:47.860
and I found out later

519
00:24:47.860 --> 00:24:50.710
probably when I was going in my senior year,

520
00:24:50.710 --> 00:24:53.930
that my godfather had made a deal with the coach

521
00:24:53.930 --> 00:24:58.930
that regardless of how poor I played that first year

522
00:24:59.270 --> 00:25:00.900
that he wouldn't cut me from the team,

523
00:25:00.900 --> 00:25:02.580
he'd keep me on the team.

524
00:25:02.580 --> 00:25:07.580
And so each year I got a little better and better

525
00:25:09.280 --> 00:25:13.620
but a lot of it was because I put a lot of time

526
00:25:13.620 --> 00:25:15.300
in trying to master the skills.

527
00:25:15.300 --> 00:25:20.300
And once again, there was a little bit of a strategy there

528
00:25:21.220 --> 00:25:23.880
because if I went to the...

529
00:25:23.880 --> 00:25:25.937
The nuns did all the teaching at the school

530
00:25:25.937 --> 00:25:29.210
and so if I went to my homeroom teacher and I said,

531
00:25:29.210 --> 00:25:31.253
Sister Dolores, I have to be...

532
00:25:31.253 --> 00:25:35.030
I have to practice from 2:00 to five o'clock

533
00:25:35.030 --> 00:25:38.420
well, then she would give permission to go over to the gym.

534
00:25:38.420 --> 00:25:40.040
And even in the off season,

535
00:25:40.040 --> 00:25:41.870
I would go in the gym every day

536
00:25:41.870 --> 00:25:46.870
and work on my fundamentals and that we only had one coach.

537
00:25:48.957 --> 00:25:50.450
Our coach had played for the Redskins,

538
00:25:50.450 --> 00:25:52.520
but he coached every sport.

539
00:25:52.520 --> 00:25:56.760
He coached basketball, baseball, football, and boxing.

540
00:25:56.760 --> 00:25:58.090
And that discipline

541
00:25:58.090 --> 00:25:59.490
to kinda go the extra mile

542
00:25:59.490 --> 00:26:01.290
and practice in off hours.

543
00:26:01.290 --> 00:26:04.110
At the time, your perception was not,

544
00:26:04.110 --> 00:26:06.960
this is my ticket out or my ticket to a new life.

545
00:26:06.960 --> 00:26:08.630
What was the drive?

546
00:26:08.630 --> 00:26:10.560
Just a personal sense of wanting to be

547
00:26:10.560 --> 00:26:11.610
as good as you could be.

548
00:26:11.610 --> 00:26:12.443
Yes.

549
00:26:14.032 --> 00:26:19.032
I actually, at no time in my life, did I ever say,

550
00:26:19.360 --> 00:26:21.950
well, when I grow up, I wanna go to college.

551
00:26:21.950 --> 00:26:26.490
And never once in our household, that when I was growing up

552
00:26:26.490 --> 00:26:28.550
did anyone ever say, George, when you go grow up,

553
00:26:28.550 --> 00:26:29.930
we want you to go to college.

554
00:26:29.930 --> 00:26:31.145
I was unheard of.

555
00:26:31.145 --> 00:26:32.170
No one in our family had ever gone

556
00:26:32.170 --> 00:26:34.020
so why did they think I was gonna go?

557
00:26:35.110 --> 00:26:40.030
And so, as I moved along, I began to realize that

558
00:26:40.030 --> 00:26:44.360
participating in athletic brought some residual value

559
00:26:44.360 --> 00:26:49.360
for you and so I utilized it to help myself grow and learn

560
00:26:53.460 --> 00:26:55.960
and get exposure because I get off campus

561
00:26:55.960 --> 00:26:58.770
and I get a chance to see how the rest of the world lived

562
00:26:58.770 --> 00:27:02.650
because television was in its very formative stages then.

563
00:27:02.650 --> 00:27:05.120
And so today, a young person can...

564
00:27:05.120 --> 00:27:08.980
He has a much broader perspective of the world

565
00:27:08.980 --> 00:27:12.190
than I ever had in those days.

566
00:27:12.190 --> 00:27:17.190
But somewhere there was the hand of God, I guess,

567
00:27:17.310 --> 00:27:19.400
just pushing me along

568
00:27:19.400 --> 00:27:24.400
and being in the right place at the right time helped me.

569
00:27:24.750 --> 00:27:27.270
But I don't know if I would've ever made it

570
00:27:27.270 --> 00:27:30.620
other than the discipline of the nuns

571
00:27:30.620 --> 00:27:35.620
and the time that they invested in me and motivated me.

572
00:27:37.100 --> 00:27:41.100
And my homeroom teacher, my senior year,

573
00:27:41.100 --> 00:27:42.760
her name was Sister Emmanuel

574
00:27:42.760 --> 00:27:45.530
and we went through through the entire year

575
00:27:45.530 --> 00:27:49.420
and she would never, ever say anything positive to me.

576
00:27:49.420 --> 00:27:51.647
And then when she found out--

577
00:27:51.647 --> 00:27:52.640
She's just being a good nun.

578
00:27:52.640 --> 00:27:54.240
Yeah.

579
00:27:54.240 --> 00:27:56.730
And when she found out I was gonna get a scholarship,

580
00:27:56.730 --> 00:27:58.300
then I had...

581
00:27:58.300 --> 00:28:00.730
It started to change because then they'd made me stay

582
00:28:00.730 --> 00:28:03.600
every day after school and I'd have to go through

583
00:28:03.600 --> 00:28:08.560
two hours of Latin and they were preparing me for the test

584
00:28:08.560 --> 00:28:11.550
and teach me word derivatives and...

585
00:28:11.550 --> 00:28:13.680
For example, if it's a multiple choice,

586
00:28:13.680 --> 00:28:15.170
the first thing you do is go through

587
00:28:15.170 --> 00:28:17.490
and eliminate the answers they can't possibly be,

588
00:28:17.490 --> 00:28:18.780
so you narrow that.

589
00:28:18.780 --> 00:28:20.490
So I went through all those

590
00:28:21.370 --> 00:28:25.370
fundamental concepts of education.

591
00:28:25.370 --> 00:28:28.277
And so when I graduated I said to her, I said,

592
00:28:28.277 --> 00:28:30.587
"Sister Emmanuel," I said, "Do you realize

593
00:28:30.587 --> 00:28:32.017
"in the entire year

594
00:28:32.017 --> 00:28:35.107
"you've never said anything positive to me."

595
00:28:35.107 --> 00:28:37.987
And she looked at me and smiled, and she said,

596
00:28:37.987 --> 00:28:39.467
"Mr. Raving, do you think maybe

597
00:28:39.467 --> 00:28:41.667
"there was a method to my madness?"

598
00:28:42.777 --> 00:28:43.610
Yeah, it seemed to work, right?

599
00:28:43.610 --> 00:28:44.670
Yeah, yes.

600
00:28:44.670 --> 00:28:47.120
'Cause I was always trying to do something extra

601
00:28:47.120 --> 00:28:50.320
just so she'd say I did a good job and she never would.

602
00:28:50.320 --> 00:28:52.830
Well, you thrive under this

603
00:28:52.830 --> 00:28:54.430
Catholic education umbrella

604
00:28:54.430 --> 00:28:57.250
and then you take that not to St. Joe's but to--

605
00:28:57.250 --> 00:28:58.470
Villanova.
Villanova

606
00:28:58.470 --> 00:29:01.000
continuing in the Catholic tradition, right?

607
00:29:01.000 --> 00:29:03.990
And you become quite a standout basketball player there.

608
00:29:03.990 --> 00:29:07.280
I think what 835 rebounds or something like that

609
00:29:07.280 --> 00:29:08.320
during your career

610
00:29:09.230 --> 00:29:12.403
successful by any stretch of the imagination.

611
00:29:13.300 --> 00:29:15.200
But even then it wasn't about,

612
00:29:15.200 --> 00:29:17.190
I'm gonna become a professional basketball player.

613
00:29:17.190 --> 00:29:20.153
Professional basketball looked very different back then.

614
00:29:21.050 --> 00:29:23.160
Well, there weren't as many teams

615
00:29:24.380 --> 00:29:29.380
and so my junior and senior year we were ranked,

616
00:29:30.170 --> 00:29:31.730
nationally ranked.

617
00:29:31.730 --> 00:29:33.860
And so when the...

618
00:29:33.860 --> 00:29:37.160
They had six rounds of the NBA draft at that time

619
00:29:37.160 --> 00:29:38.920
and I got drafted in the second round

620
00:29:38.920 --> 00:29:42.070
by the Philadelphia Warriors,

621
00:29:42.070 --> 00:29:44.791
which is now the Golden State Warriors.

622
00:29:44.791 --> 00:29:48.100
And so the owner was a guy named Eddie Gottlieb

623
00:29:48.100 --> 00:29:50.410
and I went down and I met with him

624
00:29:52.130 --> 00:29:54.820
and we talked and they had a stacked line up.

625
00:29:54.820 --> 00:29:57.820
They had Paul Arizin and Woody Sauldsberry

626
00:29:57.820 --> 00:30:02.050
and Wilt Chamberlain and Guy Rodgers and (indistinct)

627
00:30:02.050 --> 00:30:04.240
those type of players, most of whom...

628
00:30:04.240 --> 00:30:06.963
Well, all went on to be in the Hall of Fame.

629
00:30:07.930 --> 00:30:12.050
And so it wasn't that they needed me, but he did say that,

630
00:30:12.050 --> 00:30:16.860
that I would be challenged to make the lineup, and he said,

631
00:30:16.860 --> 00:30:19.270
this will show you the difference in compensation.

632
00:30:19.270 --> 00:30:23.453
He said, we'd be willing to sign you for 7,500.

633
00:30:25.077 --> 00:30:27.630
And you were already working at Sunoco.

634
00:30:27.630 --> 00:30:30.813
So that time you were making more money in a corporate job

635
00:30:30.813 --> 00:30:31.730
than you were to make--
In Sunoco

636
00:30:31.730 --> 00:30:33.560
I was in a management training program

637
00:30:33.560 --> 00:30:34.780
and they were paying me...

638
00:30:34.780 --> 00:30:36.400
They were paying me more than that

639
00:30:36.400 --> 00:30:38.960
in the management training program.

640
00:30:38.960 --> 00:30:41.040
And it wasn't something that

641
00:30:41.040 --> 00:30:44.210
I could ever see myself making a career out of it.

642
00:30:44.210 --> 00:30:45.100
And so...

643
00:30:46.310 --> 00:30:47.810
And as I said, very...

644
00:30:47.810 --> 00:30:52.810
I don't know one young man my age whoever said to me

645
00:30:52.820 --> 00:30:57.820
man, if I can just make the NBA, I'd be really happy.

646
00:30:57.920 --> 00:31:02.690
No, that wasn't a destination point for blacks in those days

647
00:31:03.887 --> 00:31:06.110
to play in the NBA.

648
00:31:06.110 --> 00:31:07.420
And what...

649
00:31:07.420 --> 00:31:12.420
I had an older cousin who went to Dartmouth

650
00:31:12.780 --> 00:31:15.050
and then on to Georgetown Law School

651
00:31:15.050 --> 00:31:17.650
and subsequently became a federal judge.

652
00:31:17.650 --> 00:31:19.770
And he was kinda my role model

653
00:31:19.770 --> 00:31:21.920
before I knew what a role model was

654
00:31:21.920 --> 00:31:24.810
and so I always wanted to be like him.

655
00:31:24.810 --> 00:31:28.920
And so he wore khaki pants all the time

656
00:31:28.920 --> 00:31:30.310
so I wore khaki pants.

657
00:31:30.310 --> 00:31:32.030
So whatever he did, I did.

658
00:31:32.030 --> 00:31:35.100
So my idea was, okay, when I graduate,

659
00:31:35.100 --> 00:31:38.230
I'll go to law school and that was gonna...

660
00:31:38.230 --> 00:31:40.030
That was my pursuit.

661
00:31:40.030 --> 00:31:44.500
But what's even more interesting when I look back on it

662
00:31:44.500 --> 00:31:49.070
before I got to be a senior

663
00:31:49.070 --> 00:31:52.300
and the scholarship came into play,

664
00:31:52.300 --> 00:31:56.180
I can distinctly remember our school set up on a bluff

665
00:31:56.180 --> 00:31:58.800
and down at the bottom was a highway

666
00:31:58.800 --> 00:32:01.540
and the Greyhound bus came by there and so

667
00:32:01.540 --> 00:32:03.550
that was how I'd get on the Greyhound bus

668
00:32:03.550 --> 00:32:05.891
and take that long journey

669
00:32:05.891 --> 00:32:07.473
from Pennsylvania on the Greyhound bus

670
00:32:07.473 --> 00:32:10.290
till I got to Washington, DC.

671
00:32:10.290 --> 00:32:12.600
And so I remember sitting up there

672
00:32:12.600 --> 00:32:14.340
waiting for the bus to come

673
00:32:17.456 --> 00:32:18.970
and I was thinking to myself,

674
00:32:18.970 --> 00:32:22.310
boy, if I can just become a pilot in the air force,

675
00:32:22.310 --> 00:32:23.370
I'll have it made.

676
00:32:23.370 --> 00:32:26.330
And that was the extent of my dreams then

677
00:32:26.330 --> 00:32:29.720
was to go into the air force and try to become a pilot.

678
00:32:29.720 --> 00:32:33.283
I had no idea that I was ever gonna go to college.

679
00:32:35.060 --> 00:32:36.863
But fate has a different path for you.

680
00:32:36.863 --> 00:32:38.380
Yes.
And nonetheless,

681
00:32:38.380 --> 00:32:40.610
you get pulled back into basketball

682
00:32:40.610 --> 00:32:43.560
first as an assistant coach at Villanova

683
00:32:43.560 --> 00:32:46.200
while you were still working your job, right?

684
00:32:46.200 --> 00:32:47.468
Yes.
Your story is like...

685
00:32:47.468 --> 00:32:48.700
It was like (indistinct).
(indistinct)...

686
00:32:48.700 --> 00:32:52.080
I might have been the original part-time assistant, but--

687
00:32:52.080 --> 00:32:53.300
And were you the first?

688
00:32:53.300 --> 00:32:55.950
This starts like this wave of you being

689
00:32:55.950 --> 00:32:58.160
the first of in many situations.

690
00:32:58.160 --> 00:33:01.390
So you were the first African American assistant coach

691
00:33:01.390 --> 00:33:03.020
in the ACC, yes?

692
00:33:03.020 --> 00:33:03.853
Yeah, I was...

693
00:33:03.853 --> 00:33:07.970
Actually I was the first a black assistant coach

694
00:33:07.970 --> 00:33:11.370
at a predominantly white, white college,

695
00:33:11.370 --> 00:33:13.300
but we didn't have all the conferences.

696
00:33:13.300 --> 00:33:16.077
There was no Big Eastern there was an ACC,

697
00:33:16.920 --> 00:33:18.060
but there was no...

698
00:33:19.040 --> 00:33:21.747
There weren't a perforation of conferences like.

699
00:33:21.747 --> 00:33:23.620
So was Villanova part of the ACC then or?

700
00:33:23.620 --> 00:33:26.760
At that time Villanova was basically

701
00:33:28.501 --> 00:33:31.380
in a make believe conference.

702
00:33:31.380 --> 00:33:32.680
And I say that because

703
00:33:32.680 --> 00:33:37.220
it didn't have a real conference structure to it,

704
00:33:37.220 --> 00:33:40.520
but it was called the Big Five and the Big Five was,

705
00:33:40.520 --> 00:33:44.420
La Salle, St. Joe's, Temple, Penn, and Villanova.

706
00:33:44.420 --> 00:33:49.420
And so that was if those schools in Philadelphia

707
00:33:52.060 --> 00:33:55.610
competed against each other and whoever had the best record,

708
00:33:55.610 --> 00:33:57.530
they were the champion of the Big Five.

709
00:33:57.530 --> 00:34:00.930
But basically in those days the Catholic schools

710
00:34:00.930 --> 00:34:04.060
were independent, what we call independent today.

711
00:34:04.060 --> 00:34:08.230
But most Catholic schools particularly

712
00:34:08.230 --> 00:34:11.770
on the Eastern seaboard,

713
00:34:11.770 --> 00:34:14.610
all had outstanding basketball teams.

714
00:34:14.610 --> 00:34:18.080
Whatever school you name from Canisius to Villanova

715
00:34:18.080 --> 00:34:21.520
to St. Joe's to Georgetown to Loyola,

716
00:34:21.520 --> 00:34:25.230
they all had great basketball teams.

717
00:34:25.230 --> 00:34:27.740
So you must have been how old at this time,

718
00:34:27.740 --> 00:34:29.950
like 25 or something?

719
00:34:29.950 --> 00:34:31.390
Yeah, I graduated...

720
00:34:31.390 --> 00:34:33.227
I went to Villanova '50, '60, '60

721
00:34:33.227 --> 00:34:36.760
and I was 20 when I graduated from Villanova.

722
00:34:36.760 --> 00:34:37.750
So I was...

723
00:34:38.940 --> 00:34:39.773
You're right,

724
00:34:39.773 --> 00:34:40.800
I'd be right around 25.

725
00:34:40.800 --> 00:34:43.470
Right, so that's probably like around 1962--

726
00:34:43.470 --> 00:34:44.980
Yes.
Or something like that.

727
00:34:44.980 --> 00:34:48.640
So this is all happening set against the backdrop of

728
00:34:48.640 --> 00:34:51.370
a pretty tumultuous time in our history

729
00:34:51.370 --> 00:34:53.010
with the civil rights movement

730
00:34:53.010 --> 00:34:54.980
and what was going on down in Selma

731
00:34:54.980 --> 00:34:57.280
with Dr. King, et cetera.

732
00:34:57.280 --> 00:35:00.330
And you start to get interested in this movement

733
00:35:00.330 --> 00:35:02.270
by a way of a friend and a family friend,

734
00:35:02.270 --> 00:35:06.330
who kind of initiate you into these conversations

735
00:35:06.330 --> 00:35:09.350
about sort of race in America,

736
00:35:09.350 --> 00:35:13.990
which then leads you to show up on the mall in 1963,

737
00:35:15.300 --> 00:35:17.410
to hear Dr. King.

738
00:35:17.410 --> 00:35:20.305
So walk us through this extraordinary experience

739
00:35:20.305 --> 00:35:21.138
that you have.

740
00:35:21.138 --> 00:35:23.210
Well, this is one of those situations--

741
00:35:23.210 --> 00:35:24.283
I know you've told this story

742
00:35:24.283 --> 00:35:25.116
a million times

743
00:35:25.116 --> 00:35:28.126
but I'm gonna make you do it.

744
00:35:28.126 --> 00:35:32.140
That I characterized earlier when I said that

745
00:35:32.140 --> 00:35:34.290
being in the right place at the right time.

746
00:35:35.630 --> 00:35:39.000
On a Wednesday night, my best friend,

747
00:35:39.000 --> 00:35:40.590
he lived in Claymont, Delaware

748
00:35:40.590 --> 00:35:42.860
and Claymont Delaware is right on the border

749
00:35:43.776 --> 00:35:47.261
of Pennsylvania and from (indistinct) over to Claymont

750
00:35:47.261 --> 00:35:49.870
is maybe an hour and some change.

751
00:35:49.870 --> 00:35:52.430
And so I was down at their house for dinner

752
00:35:54.748 --> 00:35:56.240
and my friend's name was Warren Wilson

753
00:35:56.240 --> 00:36:00.050
and his dad was a very prominent black, Dennison Wilmington.

754
00:36:00.050 --> 00:36:03.143
And so we were having dinner and in those days,

755
00:36:04.650 --> 00:36:08.877
families always gathered for the evening meal

756
00:36:08.877 --> 00:36:12.270
and there was always a conversation.

757
00:36:12.270 --> 00:36:17.260
And so Dr. Wilson asked us if we were gonna go

758
00:36:17.260 --> 00:36:20.910
to the March on Washington that weekend, and we said, "No."

759
00:36:20.910 --> 00:36:22.030
And he asked us, "Why not?"

760
00:36:22.030 --> 00:36:25.240
He said, "This is gonna be a historic moment.

761
00:36:25.240 --> 00:36:26.650
"You guys should be there."

762
00:36:26.650 --> 00:36:29.490
And so we gave him one of those adolescent excuses

763
00:36:29.490 --> 00:36:31.290
we didn't have any money or way to get there.

764
00:36:31.290 --> 00:36:32.810
So he said, "Well, I can fix that."

765
00:36:32.810 --> 00:36:36.140
So he let us use the car and he gave us money

766
00:36:36.140 --> 00:36:36.973
to go down there.

767
00:36:36.973 --> 00:36:38.940
So that Friday we hopped in the car

768
00:36:38.940 --> 00:36:43.940
and we drove down to DC and we got a motel room on...

769
00:36:47.210 --> 00:36:48.120
At that time there was...

770
00:36:48.120 --> 00:36:52.350
Route 1 was the only (indistinct) in and out of DC

771
00:36:52.350 --> 00:36:53.960
going north and south.

772
00:36:53.960 --> 00:36:58.000
And so we found a hotel room or motel room

773
00:36:58.000 --> 00:36:59.830
on New York Avenue and we decided that

774
00:36:59.830 --> 00:37:02.280
we'd drive down that evening, Friday evening

775
00:37:02.280 --> 00:37:05.170
and just get our bearings and see what was going on.

776
00:37:05.170 --> 00:37:07.090
And while we were down there,

777
00:37:07.090 --> 00:37:08.517
we ran into this guy and he said,

778
00:37:08.517 --> 00:37:10.240
"Hey, are you coming tomorrow?"

779
00:37:10.240 --> 00:37:11.891
And we said, "Yes."

780
00:37:11.891 --> 00:37:12.724
And he said,

781
00:37:12.724 --> 00:37:15.270
"Well, would you guys be interested in volunteering?"

782
00:37:15.270 --> 00:37:18.290
And we said, "For what?"

783
00:37:18.290 --> 00:37:20.677
He said, "Oh, we're gonna have twice as many people

784
00:37:20.677 --> 00:37:22.247
"as we thought we were gonna have.

785
00:37:22.247 --> 00:37:25.327
"And so we're gonna have to have a double security

786
00:37:25.327 --> 00:37:26.907
"so we're looking for volunteers.

787
00:37:26.907 --> 00:37:28.150
"Would you guys wanna volunteer?"

788
00:37:28.150 --> 00:37:29.340
And we said, "Yes."

789
00:37:29.340 --> 00:37:33.240
So he said, "Be down here tomorrow morning at 9:00."

790
00:37:33.240 --> 00:37:35.930
And we woke up early and got down there

791
00:37:35.930 --> 00:37:39.290
and we got down there about 8:00, maybe 8:15,

792
00:37:39.290 --> 00:37:41.320
and we found them and he said, "Wow, you guys--"

793
00:37:41.320 --> 00:37:42.520
Like right on the--

794
00:37:42.520 --> 00:37:43.591
On the mall.

795
00:37:43.591 --> 00:37:44.424
Yeah, in the mall...

796
00:37:44.424 --> 00:37:48.600
Yeah, the mall there where the reflecting pool in that is.

797
00:37:48.600 --> 00:37:50.490
And so he said, "You guys are here early."

798
00:37:50.490 --> 00:37:54.117
He said, "Well, we're gonna double the security up

799
00:37:54.117 --> 00:37:58.140
"at the speakers podium 'cause they had people speaking."

800
00:37:58.140 --> 00:38:00.950
John Lewis who's now in Congress

801
00:38:00.950 --> 00:38:02.830
was one of the early speakers that day.

802
00:38:02.830 --> 00:38:04.630
So it started from 9:00 in the morning

803
00:38:04.630 --> 00:38:07.500
and King was the last speaker late in the evening

804
00:38:07.500 --> 00:38:09.080
around 5:00.

805
00:38:09.080 --> 00:38:10.480
All day people speaking.

806
00:38:10.480 --> 00:38:11.710
Yes, there's one speaker--

807
00:38:11.710 --> 00:38:14.010
And everyone had like six minutes, right?

808
00:38:14.010 --> 00:38:15.170
No, five minutes.
Five minutes.

809
00:38:15.170 --> 00:38:17.940
Everybody was limited to five minutes and they...

810
00:38:17.940 --> 00:38:20.490
You had to give them

811
00:38:20.490 --> 00:38:24.060
an advanced copy of your speech for approval

812
00:38:24.060 --> 00:38:27.653
and they made John Lewis change his about 10 times.

813
00:38:28.920 --> 00:38:32.190
Baldwin, James Baldwin refused to change his

814
00:38:32.190 --> 00:38:35.310
and so they wouldn't let Baldwin speak.

815
00:38:35.310 --> 00:38:40.310
And so even King, the original speech was five minutes.

816
00:38:42.420 --> 00:38:47.420
And so what happened was there was this security structure

817
00:38:49.710 --> 00:38:53.520
and particularly for King as soon as he was done speaking,

818
00:38:53.520 --> 00:38:55.940
we were to surround and golf him

819
00:38:55.940 --> 00:38:59.530
and take him out the back way and we had an exit plan

820
00:38:59.530 --> 00:39:04.530
if there was some kind of over demonstration or violence.

821
00:39:04.760 --> 00:39:05.690
Were you up on the...

822
00:39:05.690 --> 00:39:09.165
By the podium the whole day or just for Dr. King?

823
00:39:09.165 --> 00:39:10.520
(indistinct) pictures.

824
00:39:10.520 --> 00:39:12.670
No, I was up there the whole day

825
00:39:14.023 --> 00:39:16.830
and we got a break every now and then

826
00:39:16.830 --> 00:39:19.103
to go grab a...

827
00:39:19.103 --> 00:39:20.220
There was a place in the back

828
00:39:20.220 --> 00:39:23.170
where you could go and get a sandwich and something

829
00:39:23.170 --> 00:39:24.300
called (indistinct)

830
00:39:24.300 --> 00:39:28.530
but basically I was on duty the entire time.

831
00:39:28.530 --> 00:39:32.157
And so throughout the course of the day,

832
00:39:33.730 --> 00:39:35.470
people were still coming in

833
00:39:35.470 --> 00:39:38.270
and by the time King got up to speak,

834
00:39:38.270 --> 00:39:43.270
the place was as far as you could see was human humanity.

835
00:39:43.440 --> 00:39:44.740
People...

836
00:39:44.740 --> 00:39:49.390
In fact, when we left that evening around 7:30

837
00:39:49.390 --> 00:39:52.100
to head back up to Delaware,

838
00:39:52.100 --> 00:39:55.510
the people were still trying to get into DC

839
00:39:55.510 --> 00:39:59.130
and it was over and it was bumper to bumper

840
00:39:59.130 --> 00:40:00.680
until we got Baltimore.

841
00:40:00.680 --> 00:40:02.310
People still--
Right there those...

842
00:40:02.310 --> 00:40:04.220
There's famous footage and images

843
00:40:04.220 --> 00:40:06.570
of just a sea of people all the way down

844
00:40:06.570 --> 00:40:07.403
the reflecting board.

845
00:40:07.403 --> 00:40:09.090
So King got up to speak

846
00:40:11.396 --> 00:40:15.097
and he was going through his prepared text

847
00:40:16.240 --> 00:40:20.870
and he gets to a point when I would suggest

848
00:40:20.870 --> 00:40:23.060
he's probably three quarters of the way through

849
00:40:23.060 --> 00:40:24.310
the prepared text

850
00:40:24.310 --> 00:40:26.877
and you hear a female voice say,

851
00:40:26.877 --> 00:40:28.557
"Tell them about the dream, Martin.

852
00:40:28.557 --> 00:40:30.390
"Tell them about the dream."

853
00:40:30.390 --> 00:40:35.390
Was seated behind King were Mahalia Jackson

854
00:40:35.970 --> 00:40:37.480
who most people would suggest

855
00:40:37.480 --> 00:40:39.940
is the greatest gospel singer of all time.

856
00:40:39.940 --> 00:40:43.410
And Marion Anderson she had sang the national anthem.

857
00:40:43.410 --> 00:40:48.410
And so it was Mahalia Jackson who had encouraged Martin

858
00:40:49.560 --> 00:40:51.140
to tell about the dream.

859
00:40:51.140 --> 00:40:52.370
And so what was the dream?

860
00:40:52.370 --> 00:40:53.490
The dream part

861
00:40:55.373 --> 00:40:59.320
King had used in the speech in Detroit earlier that week

862
00:40:59.320 --> 00:41:01.570
and he had used it in Selma

863
00:41:01.570 --> 00:41:04.560
and Mahalia Jackson was on both of those programs

864
00:41:04.560 --> 00:41:08.450
so she was familiar with it and obviously inspired by it.

865
00:41:08.450 --> 00:41:12.860
And so at this point, King was the last speaker

866
00:41:12.860 --> 00:41:14.997
and some people would say that he was the keynote speaker

867
00:41:14.997 --> 00:41:15.890
but that wasn't true.

868
00:41:15.890 --> 00:41:17.020
They put King last

869
00:41:17.020 --> 00:41:19.920
because they know he would hold the crowd.

870
00:41:19.920 --> 00:41:24.720
And so at that point, he deviates from the speech

871
00:41:24.720 --> 00:41:28.780
and ad libs in the "I Have A Dream" portion.

872
00:41:28.780 --> 00:41:31.940
And as a result was he was the only speaker

873
00:41:31.940 --> 00:41:35.780
who went past the five minute mark.

874
00:41:35.780 --> 00:41:36.630
Was there a moment

875
00:41:36.630 --> 00:41:39.170
when you standing next to him knew?

876
00:41:39.170 --> 00:41:41.250
Like, was it palpable in the moment like,

877
00:41:41.250 --> 00:41:42.790
okay, he's not referring to his notes and--

878
00:41:42.790 --> 00:41:44.810
No, because I didn't have the notes.

879
00:41:44.810 --> 00:41:46.450
I didn't have the notes at that point.

880
00:41:46.450 --> 00:41:48.660
But like sort of kicking into a high gear--

881
00:41:48.660 --> 00:41:50.980
No, I think King was such

882
00:41:53.088 --> 00:41:56.320
a was a highly skilled author that he...

883
00:41:56.320 --> 00:41:59.960
You were mesmerized, your thought process certainly was.

884
00:41:59.960 --> 00:42:04.325
And particularly for me, I didn't have the knowledge

885
00:42:04.325 --> 00:42:09.325
or the analytical skills to try to dissect his speech

886
00:42:10.390 --> 00:42:12.620
in that I was just like everybody else

887
00:42:12.620 --> 00:42:15.423
I was just like being in church on Sunday, you know?

888
00:42:17.452 --> 00:42:21.430
And so once King finished,

889
00:42:22.420 --> 00:42:24.850
the place went berserk.

890
00:42:24.850 --> 00:42:28.330
And so now we started to close in

891
00:42:28.330 --> 00:42:30.373
and as you can see by the picture,

892
00:42:31.690 --> 00:42:35.810
just as he begins to finish the speech

893
00:42:35.810 --> 00:42:39.540
and is moving away from the podium, he's folding it.

894
00:42:39.540 --> 00:42:44.540
And we actually have on a video King folding the speech

895
00:42:46.477 --> 00:42:49.220
and I said to him, "Dr. King, can I have that speech?"

896
00:42:49.220 --> 00:42:50.620
And he handed it to me.

897
00:42:50.620 --> 00:42:52.130
You actually have that on video?

898
00:42:52.130 --> 00:42:54.770
Yeah, and CBS found it in the archives.

899
00:42:54.770 --> 00:42:55.890
Is it online anywhere, I'd love to see that.

900
00:42:55.890 --> 00:42:57.580
Oh yeah, it's on my website.

901
00:42:57.580 --> 00:42:59.870
Oh, it is, okay.

902
00:42:59.870 --> 00:43:01.490
If you look at the James...

903
00:43:01.490 --> 00:43:04.700
There's about five different networks

904
00:43:04.700 --> 00:43:07.310
have done documentaries, but the one with CBS,

905
00:43:07.310 --> 00:43:08.980
with James Brown, you can see it.

906
00:43:08.980 --> 00:43:09.813
Right, I'll check that out.

907
00:43:09.813 --> 00:43:10.906
And so--

908
00:43:10.906 --> 00:43:11.810
So you just spontaneously

909
00:43:11.810 --> 00:43:12.720
have this idea that--

910
00:43:12.720 --> 00:43:13.950
Yeah, I don't know why

911
00:43:13.950 --> 00:43:15.560
I'd love to some great story

912
00:43:15.560 --> 00:43:19.030
that I had this whole thing premeditated, but I didn't.

913
00:43:19.030 --> 00:43:21.430
And so just as he handed it to me,

914
00:43:21.430 --> 00:43:25.447
the rabbi who was gonna say in the benediction said to him,

915
00:43:25.447 --> 00:43:28.267
"Dr. King, that's the greatest speech I've ever heard.

916
00:43:28.267 --> 00:43:30.250
"I loved your speech."

917
00:43:30.250 --> 00:43:33.500
And so his tension is switched from me

918
00:43:33.500 --> 00:43:37.460
and his focus to the rabbi and that was the end of it.

919
00:43:37.460 --> 00:43:40.960
And I just stuck it in my pocket and then when it was over,

920
00:43:40.960 --> 00:43:42.490
we ushered him out.

921
00:43:42.490 --> 00:43:46.840
So now the leadership group, A. Philip Randolph

922
00:43:46.840 --> 00:43:50.107
and Martin Luther King and the Bayard Rustin

923
00:43:50.107 --> 00:43:53.890
and those people who led to March,

924
00:43:53.890 --> 00:43:56.310
they all go to the White House to meet the president.

925
00:43:56.310 --> 00:43:58.420
And when they walk into the Oval Office,

926
00:43:58.420 --> 00:44:01.197
President Kennedy says, "Dr. King," he says,

927
00:44:01.197 --> 00:44:03.610
"I loved your 'I Have a Dream Speech.'"

928
00:44:03.610 --> 00:44:07.820
The speech has no title, but the media picked up on it

929
00:44:07.820 --> 00:44:10.900
and called it the "I Have a Dream Speech" which was unusual

930
00:44:10.900 --> 00:44:12.250
because that really was never--

931
00:44:12.250 --> 00:44:13.317
It wasn't even in the text.

932
00:44:13.317 --> 00:44:15.052
No, it wasn't even in the text.

933
00:44:15.052 --> 00:44:20.052
And so that became the way that it was given its name

934
00:44:22.770 --> 00:44:25.110
by the media.

935
00:44:25.110 --> 00:44:30.110
But it was a just a magnificent time.

936
00:44:33.360 --> 00:44:36.380
King had spent a lot of time the night before

937
00:44:36.380 --> 00:44:38.710
writing and rewriting the speech.

938
00:44:38.710 --> 00:44:42.020
And he actually grappled with trying to figure out

939
00:44:42.020 --> 00:44:45.690
how to get the I have a dream portion in,

940
00:44:45.690 --> 00:44:47.980
but it wasn't gonna meet the timeline

941
00:44:47.980 --> 00:44:50.513
and so he ultimately left it out.

942
00:44:52.166 --> 00:44:53.030
But they let him go on for like...

943
00:44:53.030 --> 00:44:54.730
Was like 16 minutes,

944
00:44:54.730 --> 00:44:55.563
Yeah.

945
00:44:55.563 --> 00:44:57.470
Well, once he got up there at that point--

946
00:44:57.470 --> 00:44:58.303
There was no stopper.

947
00:44:58.303 --> 00:44:59.500
There was no stopping him

948
00:44:59.500 --> 00:45:03.180
and the crowd would've gone berserk if they tried to

949
00:45:03.180 --> 00:45:04.680
because they had told people that,

950
00:45:04.680 --> 00:45:07.070
hey, if you go over we're just gonna cut the mic.

951
00:45:07.070 --> 00:45:08.488
But he was the last one up anyway, right?

952
00:45:08.488 --> 00:45:09.560
Yes.

953
00:45:09.560 --> 00:45:10.670
And I mean...

954
00:45:13.174 --> 00:45:14.007
What did someone say?

955
00:45:14.007 --> 00:45:15.220
Don't mess with history.

956
00:45:15.220 --> 00:45:16.053
Yeah.

957
00:45:16.053 --> 00:45:18.770
Well, speaking of history, so this speech goes down

958
00:45:18.770 --> 00:45:21.330
as arguably one of the greatest speeches

959
00:45:21.330 --> 00:45:23.870
in American history, if not the history of humanity

960
00:45:23.870 --> 00:45:27.910
up there with J. Kennedy's inauguration speech--

961
00:45:27.910 --> 00:45:30.690
Churchill and the Gettysburg address.

962
00:45:30.690 --> 00:45:32.780
In the moment was there a sense

963
00:45:32.780 --> 00:45:35.930
of that level of momentousness?

964
00:45:35.930 --> 00:45:37.590
I don't think so because I don't know

965
00:45:37.590 --> 00:45:39.400
that black people were ever allowed

966
00:45:39.400 --> 00:45:41.420
to think in such grand terms.

967
00:45:41.420 --> 00:45:42.670
Who would've ever thought

968
00:45:45.752 --> 00:45:48.290
that there would be a moment in history

969
00:45:48.290 --> 00:45:52.130
when there was an evaluation of speeches

970
00:45:53.775 --> 00:45:57.750
that moved the nation and moved the nation's conscious

971
00:45:57.750 --> 00:46:00.163
and one of them would be by a black person.

972
00:46:01.790 --> 00:46:05.983
We have to also be mindful that it took 50 years.

973
00:46:08.370 --> 00:46:10.253
Malcolm X said one time that,

974
00:46:11.095 --> 00:46:14.350
history is best situated to reward all man's deeds.

975
00:46:14.350 --> 00:46:16.500
And so my interpretation in that

976
00:46:16.500 --> 00:46:18.640
is history is best situated

977
00:46:18.640 --> 00:46:21.620
to place things in their historic context.

978
00:46:21.620 --> 00:46:24.470
So it took 50 years for that speech

979
00:46:24.470 --> 00:46:28.490
to really be evaluated accurately

980
00:46:28.490 --> 00:46:30.730
and placed in the historic context

981
00:46:30.730 --> 00:46:32.510
as it related to other speeches

982
00:46:33.703 --> 00:46:38.703
that had a meaningful effect on our social system

983
00:46:39.010 --> 00:46:40.240
and our values.

984
00:46:40.240 --> 00:46:42.830
And meanwhile, you have the actual text,

985
00:46:42.830 --> 00:46:45.060
you stick it in a book that was...

986
00:46:45.060 --> 00:46:46.920
Yeah, I had the actual speech.

987
00:46:46.920 --> 00:46:48.710
Harry Truman's biography that--

988
00:46:48.710 --> 00:46:49.830
It's right...

989
00:46:49.830 --> 00:46:52.560
The book is right behind you in the bookcase, yeah.

990
00:46:52.560 --> 00:46:55.180
So what happened my senior year in college,

991
00:46:55.180 --> 00:46:58.100
I played an East West All Star game in Kansas City

992
00:46:58.100 --> 00:47:00.820
and they took us out to Independence Missouri

993
00:47:00.820 --> 00:47:02.930
to meet President Truman.

994
00:47:02.930 --> 00:47:05.210
And when we were out there,

995
00:47:05.210 --> 00:47:08.750
when he was done visiting with us, he had a (indistinct)

996
00:47:08.750 --> 00:47:12.870
above three tables with books stacked high on him.

997
00:47:12.870 --> 00:47:17.870
And he had written his two volume set of on his presidency.

998
00:47:21.580 --> 00:47:25.050
And so he gave each of us a set

999
00:47:25.050 --> 00:47:26.840
and mine says to George Raveling...

1000
00:47:26.840 --> 00:47:31.840
It's got the date 1960 and to George Raveling

1001
00:47:32.010 --> 00:47:35.480
from Harry S. Truman and both of them were signed like that.

1002
00:47:35.480 --> 00:47:37.930
So I put the speech inside of it

1003
00:47:37.930 --> 00:47:40.480
because I knew one, I'd remember

1004
00:47:40.480 --> 00:47:43.940
two, that I would never throw those books away

1005
00:47:43.940 --> 00:47:45.230
because how many people can say

1006
00:47:45.230 --> 00:47:47.210
that they have a personally autographed book

1007
00:47:47.210 --> 00:47:49.650
by the president of the United States.

1008
00:47:49.650 --> 00:47:50.483
And so it's--

1009
00:47:50.483 --> 00:47:51.316
But you actually

1010
00:47:51.316 --> 00:47:53.190
don't end up really remembering it, right?

1011
00:47:53.190 --> 00:47:54.241
It sort of escapes your memory--

1012
00:47:54.241 --> 00:47:55.074
No.

1013
00:47:55.074 --> 00:47:56.440
So for 25 years...

1014
00:47:56.440 --> 00:47:59.400
For 25 years, I never thought about it

1015
00:47:59.400 --> 00:48:00.730
and my wife didn't even...

1016
00:48:00.730 --> 00:48:02.570
No one knew I had it.

1017
00:48:02.570 --> 00:48:05.900
And I never talked about it because at that point

1018
00:48:05.900 --> 00:48:09.520
it hadn't taken on its historic significance.

1019
00:48:09.520 --> 00:48:11.300
Even after the assassination?

1020
00:48:11.300 --> 00:48:12.930
No.

1021
00:48:12.930 --> 00:48:13.763
When I...

1022
00:48:13.763 --> 00:48:17.080
How it became public was when I went from

1023
00:48:18.880 --> 00:48:21.940
Washington state as head basketball coach to Iowa.

1024
00:48:21.940 --> 00:48:23.140
When I got to Iowa,

1025
00:48:23.140 --> 00:48:26.303
obviously the first black head coach there.

1026
00:48:27.210 --> 00:48:31.750
And so at that time, most of the major newspapers

1027
00:48:31.750 --> 00:48:35.487
had a Sunday magazine section that came with it

1028
00:48:35.487 --> 00:48:38.220
and the New York Times is one of the few papers

1029
00:48:38.220 --> 00:48:41.130
that still have their own.

1030
00:48:41.130 --> 00:48:43.710
And so the the Cedar Rapids Gazette

1031
00:48:43.710 --> 00:48:46.720
sent a reporter down to do this story on me

1032
00:48:46.720 --> 00:48:50.060
and so as going through the questions,

1033
00:48:50.060 --> 00:48:52.833
I call it a throwaway question he just said to me,

1034
00:48:53.827 --> 00:48:56.770
"Coach, were you ever involved in a civil rights movement?"

1035
00:48:56.770 --> 00:48:58.890
And I said, "Kinda," and he said, "What do you mean kinda?"

1036
00:48:58.890 --> 00:49:00.550
And I explained to him what happened.

1037
00:49:00.550 --> 00:49:02.987
And he said, "Oh my God, you have the speech.

1038
00:49:02.987 --> 00:49:03.820
"Where is it?"

1039
00:49:03.820 --> 00:49:06.030
And I said, "It's down in the basement in some boxes,"

1040
00:49:06.030 --> 00:49:08.300
'cause I'd only been there about two or three weeks

1041
00:49:08.300 --> 00:49:10.040
and I hadn't unpacked yet.

1042
00:49:10.040 --> 00:49:12.817
And so we go down in the basement and we find it

1043
00:49:12.817 --> 00:49:15.130
and I thought the guy was gonna have a heart attack.

1044
00:49:15.130 --> 00:49:20.130
He was hyperventilating and then he called the sports editor

1045
00:49:21.350 --> 00:49:24.430
and said, "Oh my God, you can't believe what happened."

1046
00:49:24.430 --> 00:49:28.100
And he asked, was it okay to send a photographer down,

1047
00:49:28.100 --> 00:49:30.040
to take a picture of it.

1048
00:49:30.040 --> 00:49:34.900
And so that was the first time that anyone knew

1049
00:49:34.900 --> 00:49:36.850
that I had the speech.

1050
00:49:36.850 --> 00:49:41.060
But even then it didn't cause a gigantic uproar.

1051
00:49:41.060 --> 00:49:44.030
I used to keep it on the wall in my office

1052
00:49:45.880 --> 00:49:49.830
but then once it became more...

1053
00:49:50.740 --> 00:49:53.550
The government officially anointed

1054
00:49:53.550 --> 00:49:57.850
a day for Martin Luther King Day, now people,

1055
00:49:57.850 --> 00:49:59.670
the story was getting around.

1056
00:49:59.670 --> 00:50:02.730
ESPN had it on, Digger Phelps would always bring it up

1057
00:50:02.730 --> 00:50:07.730
on a program that Coach Raveling has the original speech.

1058
00:50:08.470 --> 00:50:11.340
And so more and more people started to understand

1059
00:50:11.340 --> 00:50:12.930
that I had it.

1060
00:50:12.930 --> 00:50:16.210
And my wife got a little nervous because she felt

1061
00:50:16.210 --> 00:50:17.957
you travel a lot--

1062
00:50:18.827 --> 00:50:19.660
You could get the house broken into.

1063
00:50:19.660 --> 00:50:21.230
Yeah, somebody had break into the house

1064
00:50:21.230 --> 00:50:24.570
so she said, we gotta move it out and put it in the bank.

1065
00:50:24.570 --> 00:50:25.430
Or take it out of the book.

1066
00:50:25.430 --> 00:50:26.263
Yeah.

1067
00:50:26.263 --> 00:50:29.940
And so we moved it out of the house because...

1068
00:50:29.940 --> 00:50:32.730
And she's always said back in those days,

1069
00:50:32.730 --> 00:50:35.170
make sure you keep saying publicly it's not in the house

1070
00:50:35.170 --> 00:50:37.750
because I don't want somebody breaking into our house

1071
00:50:37.750 --> 00:50:39.070
and trying to steal.

1072
00:50:39.070 --> 00:50:40.500
But it would be...

1073
00:50:40.500 --> 00:50:42.730
If it were still in the house now

1074
00:50:42.730 --> 00:50:44.700
there would be a more likelihood that

1075
00:50:44.700 --> 00:50:47.530
someone would probably try to into break to the house now

1076
00:50:47.530 --> 00:50:52.103
because we live in such an unstable environment.

1077
00:50:55.033 --> 00:50:57.330
But in those days,

1078
00:50:57.330 --> 00:51:00.700
I don't know that there was any real threat to us

1079
00:51:02.780 --> 00:51:04.100
and people weren't really...

1080
00:51:04.100 --> 00:51:06.330
I mean, such a large city like Los Angeles,

1081
00:51:06.330 --> 00:51:09.210
people weren't even sure where I lived.

1082
00:51:09.210 --> 00:51:10.960
So over the years,

1083
00:51:10.960 --> 00:51:12.290
I'm sure there's been suitors,

1084
00:51:12.290 --> 00:51:13.900
people trying to buy this from you,

1085
00:51:13.900 --> 00:51:16.690
estimates run anywhere from a couple million bucks

1086
00:51:16.690 --> 00:51:19.700
all the way up to like $25 million.

1087
00:51:19.700 --> 00:51:22.233
You've repeatedly refused to sell it.

1088
00:51:23.341 --> 00:51:24.174
But I would...

1089
00:51:24.174 --> 00:51:26.147
And I can understand the reasons behind that

1090
00:51:26.147 --> 00:51:28.940
and I wanna dig into that a little bit more deeply,

1091
00:51:28.940 --> 00:51:31.023
but it must have occurred to you,

1092
00:51:32.110 --> 00:51:36.090
why not share it with the Smithsonian, put it on loan

1093
00:51:36.090 --> 00:51:38.940
so everybody can enjoy it.

1094
00:51:38.940 --> 00:51:39.860
I agree with you

1095
00:51:39.860 --> 00:51:41.850
and that's exactly what we're gonna do.

1096
00:51:41.850 --> 00:51:45.470
I had been consistently saying that

1097
00:51:45.470 --> 00:51:47.720
I was gonna leave it to my son when I die

1098
00:51:47.720 --> 00:51:49.510
and that still my intention.

1099
00:51:49.510 --> 00:51:50.460
But even--

1100
00:51:50.460 --> 00:51:51.293
So long as he doesn't sell it.

1101
00:51:51.293 --> 00:51:52.600
Yes, so long as he doesn't sell it.

1102
00:51:52.600 --> 00:51:56.400
And so about two years ago,

1103
00:51:56.400 --> 00:52:00.930
the gentleman who was the producer of the CBS documentary

1104
00:52:00.930 --> 00:52:05.200
on me having this speech is a young man named Alvin Patrick.

1105
00:52:05.200 --> 00:52:07.760
And he had encouraged me to go down

1106
00:52:07.760 --> 00:52:12.210
and meet with the people at the New African American Museum,

1107
00:52:12.210 --> 00:52:14.410
which is part of the Smithsonian.

1108
00:52:14.410 --> 00:52:16.047
And I went down and I met with them

1109
00:52:16.047 --> 00:52:19.490
and they were very gracious and it is our intention

1110
00:52:19.490 --> 00:52:21.110
to get...

1111
00:52:21.110 --> 00:52:24.400
I think it just opened a few weeks ago.

1112
00:52:24.400 --> 00:52:25.310
And so it's our--

1113
00:52:25.310 --> 00:52:27.270
Oh, it's that beautiful new...

1114
00:52:27.270 --> 00:52:28.103
They just built it, right?

1115
00:52:28.103 --> 00:52:29.155
Yes.

1116
00:52:29.155 --> 00:52:29.988
I was just in Washington last week,

1117
00:52:29.988 --> 00:52:30.821
I saw it.

1118
00:52:30.821 --> 00:52:31.810
It's magnificent looking building.

1119
00:52:31.810 --> 00:52:35.130
And so ultimately within the next 12 months

1120
00:52:35.130 --> 00:52:36.470
it'll be housed there.

1121
00:52:36.470 --> 00:52:38.790
Oh, that's fantastic, that's great.

1122
00:52:38.790 --> 00:52:42.930
So I can't help, but wonder,

1123
00:52:42.930 --> 00:52:46.240
you keep characterizing this as right place, right time,

1124
00:52:46.240 --> 00:52:49.660
but on some level it almost seems faded.

1125
00:52:49.660 --> 00:52:54.660
You are this sort of paradigm breaking person in sports

1126
00:52:54.770 --> 00:52:58.420
in that being the first African American

1127
00:52:58.420 --> 00:52:59.540
assistant coach at Villanova

1128
00:52:59.540 --> 00:53:02.720
and then going on to coach at University of Washington

1129
00:53:02.720 --> 00:53:03.553
and then--
Washington state.

1130
00:53:03.553 --> 00:53:05.290
Washington state, Iowa--

1131
00:53:05.290 --> 00:53:06.870
And USC.
And USC.

1132
00:53:06.870 --> 00:53:09.250
And at each one of those sort of train stops

1133
00:53:09.250 --> 00:53:11.853
being the first African American coach.

1134
00:53:12.780 --> 00:53:16.090
Again, set against this kind of interesting time

1135
00:53:16.090 --> 00:53:17.990
in the evolution of civil rights

1136
00:53:17.990 --> 00:53:21.180
and then being the sort of

1137
00:53:24.040 --> 00:53:28.650
repository of this landmark speech on some level

1138
00:53:28.650 --> 00:53:30.280
doesn't the message seep through to you.

1139
00:53:30.280 --> 00:53:32.150
Like, I'm...

1140
00:53:32.150 --> 00:53:34.070
There's a reason behind all of this.

1141
00:53:34.070 --> 00:53:36.710
Like what is the responsibility that you feel

1142
00:53:36.710 --> 00:53:39.580
to speak to these issues

1143
00:53:39.580 --> 00:53:43.140
as somebody who is carrying on in the tradition of Dr. King

1144
00:53:43.140 --> 00:53:48.140
and somebody who has himself broken through barriers?

1145
00:53:48.190 --> 00:53:53.190
Well, I'd think that with each passing year,

1146
00:53:53.590 --> 00:53:58.260
I became much more aware of what was

1147
00:54:00.030 --> 00:54:02.880
the larger world around me

1148
00:54:04.450 --> 00:54:09.450
and I grew to understand that we lived out the cliches

1149
00:54:12.090 --> 00:54:14.800
of the last hired and the first fired

1150
00:54:14.800 --> 00:54:17.600
that we live in ghettos.

1151
00:54:17.600 --> 00:54:20.710
When I was growing up, I never even realized

1152
00:54:20.710 --> 00:54:22.670
I lived in a ghetto until I got to Villanova

1153
00:54:22.670 --> 00:54:24.440
and took a sociology class.

1154
00:54:24.440 --> 00:54:29.440
And I mean I was perfectly happy with my life

1155
00:54:30.870 --> 00:54:33.323
even though most of it was in a boarding school.

1156
00:54:34.610 --> 00:54:38.477
But as I grew older, I began to put my life

1157
00:54:40.190 --> 00:54:45.190
and my pursuits in a broader context and I realized that

1158
00:54:45.650 --> 00:54:50.650
that one of the impediments that I was gonna carry with me

1159
00:54:51.040 --> 00:54:54.200
for the rest of my life was my pigmentation

1160
00:54:54.200 --> 00:54:57.330
and people were gonna judge me differently.

1161
00:54:57.330 --> 00:55:01.340
But over the years, I also grew to understand that,

1162
00:55:01.340 --> 00:55:04.920
that the one thing that most people

1163
00:55:04.920 --> 00:55:08.460
respect about another person is their intellect,

1164
00:55:08.460 --> 00:55:11.950
whether you're black, white, whatever.

1165
00:55:11.950 --> 00:55:15.140
They might still have misgivings about you

1166
00:55:15.140 --> 00:55:18.710
but they will respect your intellect.

1167
00:55:18.710 --> 00:55:23.710
And so I try to compensate by being an information gatherer

1168
00:55:26.130 --> 00:55:29.940
a knowledge gatherer trying to learn as much as I could

1169
00:55:29.940 --> 00:55:34.510
so that I could effectively compete in our social system

1170
00:55:35.439 --> 00:55:38.870
even if I had all the hardships and the drapings

1171
00:55:38.870 --> 00:55:42.053
that came with segregation or discrimination.

1172
00:55:44.420 --> 00:55:47.420
At the end of the day, it was incumbent upon me

1173
00:55:47.420 --> 00:55:51.600
to figure out how can I successfully compete

1174
00:55:51.600 --> 00:55:52.630
in this environment?

1175
00:55:52.630 --> 00:55:54.060
What are the skills I need?

1176
00:55:54.060 --> 00:55:57.500
What are the things I need to do?

1177
00:55:57.500 --> 00:56:01.670
And one of those things was obviously to be able

1178
00:56:01.670 --> 00:56:05.540
to have a courageous voice and stand up

1179
00:56:05.540 --> 00:56:08.010
and speak out against injustice.

1180
00:56:08.010 --> 00:56:13.010
Everybody can't be Malcolm X or Martin Luther King,

1181
00:56:13.050 --> 00:56:16.520
or Stokie Carmichael or Huey Newton.

1182
00:56:16.520 --> 00:56:21.050
I think there's all of us have a role to play in life

1183
00:56:21.050 --> 00:56:24.010
and we have to figure out what that role is

1184
00:56:24.010 --> 00:56:26.370
and we have to play it.

1185
00:56:26.370 --> 00:56:28.120
But at the end of the day,

1186
00:56:28.120 --> 00:56:30.860
we have to accept the responsibility

1187
00:56:33.064 --> 00:56:37.780
that it's a moral obligation to speak out against

1188
00:56:37.780 --> 00:56:39.763
injustice and inequality.

1189
00:56:40.711 --> 00:56:43.210
And here we are today

1190
00:56:44.231 --> 00:56:49.123
and it's a more sophisticated type of discrimination.

1191
00:56:51.260 --> 00:56:53.800
And it's now not something that

1192
00:56:54.710 --> 00:56:58.010
is just discrimination against a person

1193
00:56:58.010 --> 00:57:01.810
because of the pigmentation of their skin.

1194
00:57:01.810 --> 00:57:03.710
There's discrimination against women,

1195
00:57:03.710 --> 00:57:05.660
there's discrimination against gays,

1196
00:57:05.660 --> 00:57:09.383
there's discrimination against Latinos and that.

1197
00:57:10.613 --> 00:57:15.613
And so it's become a societal problem in a real sense.

1198
00:57:17.420 --> 00:57:20.940
I remember Moynihan in his report center Moynihan,

1199
00:57:20.940 --> 00:57:23.770
when he did the report on black America

1200
00:57:23.770 --> 00:57:27.200
he suggested that the number one problem in black America

1201
00:57:28.252 --> 00:57:31.850
was the broken home structure

1202
00:57:31.850 --> 00:57:36.030
that you had this ever-growing population of black people,

1203
00:57:36.030 --> 00:57:39.460
black children who were being raised

1204
00:57:39.460 --> 00:57:41.010
in single parent households

1205
00:57:41.010 --> 00:57:43.260
and because of the judicial system,

1206
00:57:43.260 --> 00:57:48.260
the courts tended to award the child to the woman,

1207
00:57:48.490 --> 00:57:49.323
the mother.

1208
00:57:49.323 --> 00:57:51.760
And so they were growing up in a home

1209
00:57:51.760 --> 00:57:55.610
without male role models, without male supervision.

1210
00:57:55.610 --> 00:57:57.960
But what we soon began to realize

1211
00:57:57.960 --> 00:57:59.380
that over the coming years,

1212
00:57:59.380 --> 00:58:01.060
it really wasn't a black problem,

1213
00:58:01.060 --> 00:58:02.340
it was an American problem

1214
00:58:02.340 --> 00:58:05.950
because we have equal amount of white children

1215
00:58:05.950 --> 00:58:08.670
who grow up in single parent households too.

1216
00:58:08.670 --> 00:58:11.833
And so over the course of time,

1217
00:58:12.934 --> 00:58:17.090
this so-called social and discriminatory systems

1218
00:58:19.550 --> 00:58:21.640
became cancerous and they grew

1219
00:58:22.490 --> 00:58:27.490
and they started to spread out beyond the black communities

1220
00:58:28.969 --> 00:58:31.117
and to white communities and to Latin communities

1221
00:58:31.117 --> 00:58:32.793
and to Asian communities.

1222
00:58:34.488 --> 00:58:37.690
And so over the years, I've watched it,

1223
00:58:37.690 --> 00:58:42.690
this racial demographic journey to here.

1224
00:58:43.870 --> 00:58:46.560
I go from Washington, DC to Los Angeles,

1225
00:58:46.560 --> 00:58:48.900
I live in a city now in Los Angeles

1226
00:58:48.900 --> 00:58:51.790
where the ethnic breakdown is the dominant ethnic group

1227
00:58:51.790 --> 00:58:55.963
in Los Angeles are Latinos, Asians, blacks, whites.

1228
00:58:56.890 --> 00:58:58.670
And so much progress has been made.

1229
00:58:58.670 --> 00:59:01.520
Here we are in the twilight of

1230
00:59:02.580 --> 00:59:04.590
the eight years of the Obama administration,

1231
00:59:04.590 --> 00:59:06.587
first African American president.

1232
00:59:07.800 --> 00:59:11.450
Professionals for the NBA, the NFL is gotta be around 80%

1233
00:59:11.450 --> 00:59:13.450
African American at this point in terms of players

1234
00:59:13.450 --> 00:59:16.920
which is a massive shift from your days of being a player

1235
00:59:16.920 --> 00:59:19.230
and perhaps the early days of you coaching.

1236
00:59:19.230 --> 00:59:23.420
And yet we're also in a social and political climate

1237
00:59:23.420 --> 00:59:26.680
that seems more divided than ever.

1238
00:59:26.680 --> 00:59:28.020
So when you think about,

1239
00:59:28.020 --> 00:59:30.723
you had mentioned earlier we all have a role to play,

1240
00:59:33.030 --> 00:59:35.540
how do you think about and articulate

1241
00:59:36.510 --> 00:59:37.380
how you see your...

1242
00:59:37.380 --> 00:59:38.630
Like what is your role?

1243
00:59:38.630 --> 00:59:40.360
Like how would you define

1244
00:59:40.360 --> 00:59:42.940
what your role in this grand play is?

1245
00:59:42.940 --> 00:59:46.050
I see myself as a servant leader.

1246
00:59:46.050 --> 00:59:49.150
I think at this juncture in my life,

1247
00:59:49.150 --> 00:59:52.710
I'm kind of on the other side of the mountain now at 79.

1248
00:59:52.710 --> 00:59:57.220
And so I fully recognize it's no longer about me,

1249
00:59:57.220 --> 00:59:58.470
it's about them.

1250
00:59:58.470 --> 01:00:03.470
And so I have to reach out and try to positively

1251
01:00:03.490 --> 01:00:05.930
affect as many lives as I can.

1252
01:00:05.930 --> 01:00:07.650
And so my mission every day is

1253
01:00:07.650 --> 01:00:09.960
to be a positive difference maker

1254
01:00:09.960 --> 01:00:13.390
and as many lives of people as I can,

1255
01:00:13.390 --> 01:00:15.500
black folks and white folks.

1256
01:00:15.500 --> 01:00:20.440
And I try to target young people and share with them

1257
01:00:23.400 --> 01:00:27.960
my life lessons and hopes that my grandma used to say,

1258
01:00:27.960 --> 01:00:30.050
if you listen to me, I can help you avoid

1259
01:00:30.050 --> 01:00:33.240
some of the bumps and potholes in the road.

1260
01:00:33.240 --> 01:00:35.150
And so that I...

1261
01:00:35.150 --> 01:00:38.360
To this day, I still feel that that's my mission

1262
01:00:38.360 --> 01:00:42.450
to help other people avoid some of the potholes in the road

1263
01:00:42.450 --> 01:00:45.637
because I've lived a life that they have to live

1264
01:00:45.637 --> 01:00:50.637
and so I'm gonna spend all of the rest of my remaining life

1265
01:00:50.780 --> 01:00:53.360
trying to be a positive difference maker

1266
01:00:53.360 --> 01:00:57.200
in as many lives as I possibly can.

1267
01:00:57.200 --> 01:01:00.690
And when I look back now in my life,

1268
01:01:00.690 --> 01:01:03.473
I've lived a variety of lives.

1269
01:01:04.920 --> 01:01:08.330
I truly believe when I look back on my life

1270
01:01:08.330 --> 01:01:10.453
that you live your life in buckets.

1271
01:01:11.610 --> 01:01:15.300
Say 10 to 20, 20 to 30, 30 to 40.

1272
01:01:15.300 --> 01:01:19.160
But when I was 40 years old,

1273
01:01:19.160 --> 01:01:22.067
I was no longer the person I was when I was 30 years old

1274
01:01:22.067 --> 01:01:26.340
and at 79 I'm not the same person I was at 69.

1275
01:01:26.340 --> 01:01:27.840
What's the biggest difference?

1276
01:01:27.840 --> 01:01:32.500
I think one, you're in a constant,

1277
01:01:32.500 --> 01:01:35.570
your values change, your interest change,

1278
01:01:35.570 --> 01:01:39.040
your health changes, your thinking changes.

1279
01:01:39.040 --> 01:01:43.770
And then you get to a point where you have no choice anymore

1280
01:01:43.770 --> 01:01:44.603
about changing.

1281
01:01:44.603 --> 01:01:49.480
Technology has ushered into my life and forced me to take on

1282
01:01:51.090 --> 01:01:53.570
a whole new existence.

1283
01:01:53.570 --> 01:01:56.670
I mean, I have to learn the language of technology,

1284
01:01:56.670 --> 01:02:00.050
I have to learn the utilization of technology,

1285
01:02:00.050 --> 01:02:03.920
I have to learn the value that it creates for me.

1286
01:02:03.920 --> 01:02:06.940
And fortunately it came at a time in my life

1287
01:02:07.790 --> 01:02:10.890
with the proper use of technology,

1288
01:02:10.890 --> 01:02:13.740
it's allowed me to remain relevant

1289
01:02:13.740 --> 01:02:18.740
in a time in my life that's very important

1290
01:02:19.000 --> 01:02:23.180
because now I'm able to transfer from old thinking

1291
01:02:26.050 --> 01:02:29.700
to new thinking, from old knowledge to new knowledge.

1292
01:02:29.700 --> 01:02:34.030
To be successful and compete in society today,

1293
01:02:34.030 --> 01:02:37.660
we have to relearn things, we have to rethink things,

1294
01:02:37.660 --> 01:02:41.730
we have to acquire new knowledge

1295
01:02:41.730 --> 01:02:44.070
that's relevant in this time.

1296
01:02:44.070 --> 01:02:45.260
I would imagine it gets...

1297
01:02:45.260 --> 01:02:47.060
It must get harder as you get older.

1298
01:02:47.060 --> 01:02:51.360
I mean, most people who are, settling into their 70s and 80s

1299
01:02:51.360 --> 01:02:52.980
are kind of the catchphrase where

1300
01:02:52.980 --> 01:02:54.830
you're just stuck in your ways like you...

1301
01:02:54.830 --> 01:02:56.980
At some point, that evolution sort of stops

1302
01:02:56.980 --> 01:03:00.120
and you're just not gonna get the new iPhone,

1303
01:03:00.120 --> 01:03:01.020
it's just not gonna happen.

1304
01:03:01.020 --> 01:03:03.680
Or you're not gonna figure out the new TV remote.

1305
01:03:03.680 --> 01:03:06.720
It's like you draw a line in the sand and you're like done,

1306
01:03:06.720 --> 01:03:09.417
but what I'm hearing you say is you are committed to--

1307
01:03:09.417 --> 01:03:12.610
You're only done if you think you're done.

1308
01:03:12.610 --> 01:03:13.490
Well, the amazing thing

1309
01:03:13.490 --> 01:03:16.260
is you have this incredibly robust website.

1310
01:03:16.260 --> 01:03:18.307
Like when I went to your website the first time I thought,

1311
01:03:18.307 --> 01:03:19.140
(indistinct)...

1312
01:03:19.140 --> 01:03:20.170
You don't have your bio.

1313
01:03:20.170 --> 01:03:22.500
I didn't expect there to be very much content

1314
01:03:22.500 --> 01:03:25.310
and yet there's all kinds of articles there

1315
01:03:25.310 --> 01:03:29.620
and podcast and video interviews and the book club

1316
01:03:29.620 --> 01:03:31.790
and here's my monthly recommendations

1317
01:03:31.790 --> 01:03:32.750
for what you should be reading,

1318
01:03:32.750 --> 01:03:36.360
like a ton of amazing, really helpful, inspiring content.

1319
01:03:36.360 --> 01:03:38.170
And I thought, wow, this guy's like really

1320
01:03:38.170 --> 01:03:39.750
with what's going on right now

1321
01:03:39.750 --> 01:03:44.750
and that must take a level of attention and energy and focus

1322
01:03:46.430 --> 01:03:48.340
that I think most people at your age

1323
01:03:48.340 --> 01:03:50.300
probably just aren't that interested in.

1324
01:03:50.300 --> 01:03:51.780
Well, I'll back to

1325
01:03:51.780 --> 01:03:56.780
what I said before is that I can control my environment now.

1326
01:03:59.600 --> 01:04:03.490
You rarely ever see me other than with...

1327
01:04:03.490 --> 01:04:05.370
And my wife is not the same age as I

1328
01:04:05.370 --> 01:04:08.200
but you will rarely ever see me hang around with

1329
01:04:08.200 --> 01:04:11.670
or associate with people that are my own age.

1330
01:04:11.670 --> 01:04:12.665
I'll...

1331
01:04:12.665 --> 01:04:13.710
'Cause you and Ryan Holiday are hanging out.

1332
01:04:13.710 --> 01:04:14.543
No.

1333
01:04:15.420 --> 01:04:17.240
I hang around with younger people

1334
01:04:17.240 --> 01:04:19.340
because they help me think young,

1335
01:04:19.340 --> 01:04:22.630
they teach me how to use technology,

1336
01:04:22.630 --> 01:04:27.120
I can listen to them and they're all...

1337
01:04:27.120 --> 01:04:29.340
The young people are my mentors.

1338
01:04:29.340 --> 01:04:30.420
They're the ones that are...

1339
01:04:30.420 --> 01:04:33.230
They're my teachers, they're my professors right now

1340
01:04:34.275 --> 01:04:35.680
and they stimulate me

1341
01:04:35.680 --> 01:04:40.410
because if I hang around with people of my own age,

1342
01:04:40.410 --> 01:04:43.920
the talk is always gonna be about the past and the now,

1343
01:04:43.920 --> 01:04:45.710
young people are talking about

1344
01:04:45.710 --> 01:04:46.990
they're looking into the future,

1345
01:04:46.990 --> 01:04:48.980
they're defining what the future is.

1346
01:04:48.980 --> 01:04:53.230
And so I find it stimulating to be around them

1347
01:04:53.230 --> 01:04:55.900
and to listen and hang onto their coattail

1348
01:04:55.900 --> 01:04:57.110
because if I hang on

1349
01:04:57.110 --> 01:04:59.620
they're gonna drag me into the future with them

1350
01:04:59.620 --> 01:05:01.223
and that's where I wanna be.

1351
01:05:02.630 --> 01:05:04.363
My biggest challenge is,

1352
01:05:07.006 --> 01:05:11.180
I'm an African American ex basketball coach, 69 years old,

1353
01:05:11.180 --> 01:05:15.583
how do I stay relevant in this contemporary time?

1354
01:05:16.712 --> 01:05:17.570
I don't...

1355
01:05:17.570 --> 01:05:19.320
I don't wanna be put in a position

1356
01:05:20.568 --> 01:05:22.990
where I'm living my life in reverse.

1357
01:05:22.990 --> 01:05:27.840
I wanna live my life going forward.

1358
01:05:27.840 --> 01:05:32.840
And so what I've done is I boiled it down to this equation

1359
01:05:33.600 --> 01:05:35.400
and this question...

1360
01:05:35.400 --> 01:05:39.990
And the question I ask myself on a consistent basis is this,

1361
01:05:39.990 --> 01:05:44.240
what is it that I don't know but I need to know

1362
01:05:44.240 --> 01:05:47.510
to remain relative and competitive and growing

1363
01:05:47.510 --> 01:05:49.940
and prospering in today's society.

1364
01:05:49.940 --> 01:05:54.070
And then I constantly search on what I don't know,

1365
01:05:54.070 --> 01:05:58.010
but what I need to know and the great part about it is,

1366
01:05:58.010 --> 01:06:01.190
is all I gotta do is go on my iPhone, hit Google,

1367
01:06:01.190 --> 01:06:03.590
and I can educate myself.

1368
01:06:03.590 --> 01:06:05.980
I told my wife the other day, I said, you know what,

1369
01:06:05.980 --> 01:06:10.910
I'm tired of hearing night after night quoting these polls.

1370
01:06:10.910 --> 01:06:12.660
And with...

1371
01:06:12.660 --> 01:06:16.140
As if we are to accept those as being valid

1372
01:06:16.140 --> 01:06:18.390
'cause I have all kind of questions, okay.

1373
01:06:18.390 --> 01:06:19.440
Who did you poll?

1374
01:06:19.440 --> 01:06:22.313
I'm 79 never had been polled once in my life.

1375
01:06:23.710 --> 01:06:25.380
How many people did you poll?

1376
01:06:25.380 --> 01:06:27.590
What were the questions that you asked?

1377
01:06:27.590 --> 01:06:30.720
What was their political affiliation?

1378
01:06:30.720 --> 01:06:31.760
And I got a lot of things.

1379
01:06:31.760 --> 01:06:34.070
So I told my wife, I said, you know what,

1380
01:06:34.070 --> 01:06:39.000
I'm gonna educate myself so that I can critically analyze

1381
01:06:39.000 --> 01:06:41.930
and think about this thing about polls

1382
01:06:41.930 --> 01:06:44.680
and I'm gonna start questioning all these assumptions

1383
01:06:44.680 --> 01:06:45.750
about polls.

1384
01:06:45.750 --> 01:06:47.720
So I Google and I...

1385
01:06:48.740 --> 01:06:53.740
I went on Google and I asked for books on political polls

1386
01:06:53.770 --> 01:06:55.360
and about 20 of them came up

1387
01:06:55.360 --> 01:06:57.920
and so I picked out five of them and I ordered them

1388
01:06:57.920 --> 01:06:58.753
and I'm gonna read it.

1389
01:06:58.753 --> 01:07:01.750
In 60 days from now,

1390
01:07:01.750 --> 01:07:04.900
I am gonna be challenging people...

1391
01:07:04.900 --> 01:07:06.260
No, I'm gonna be challenging--

1392
01:07:06.260 --> 01:07:07.093
Come November.

1393
01:07:07.093 --> 01:07:08.510
Yeah, I'm gonna challenge people's--

1394
01:07:08.510 --> 01:07:09.343
I like it.

1395
01:07:09.343 --> 01:07:10.610
And I'm gonna challenge the networks

1396
01:07:10.610 --> 01:07:11.790
when they give us this.

1397
01:07:11.790 --> 01:07:16.323
I mean, Quinnipiac or New York Times or Wall Street poll.

1398
01:07:17.610 --> 01:07:19.860
What makes their opinion any more valid

1399
01:07:19.860 --> 01:07:21.460
than your opinion or my opinion?

1400
01:07:21.460 --> 01:07:22.560
Yeah, there's a lot of science

1401
01:07:22.560 --> 01:07:24.030
that goes into crafting those questions

1402
01:07:24.030 --> 01:07:26.200
to drive a certain result.

1403
01:07:26.200 --> 01:07:27.870
Well, I could talk to you about this stuff

1404
01:07:27.870 --> 01:07:29.010
for like six hours,

1405
01:07:29.010 --> 01:07:31.380
but I know we're on a little bit of a leash here.

1406
01:07:31.380 --> 01:07:32.640
Well, we gotta do it again.

1407
01:07:32.640 --> 01:07:34.180
Well, I'm just sitting here thinking

1408
01:07:34.180 --> 01:07:35.660
while you're answering I'm like, there's no way

1409
01:07:35.660 --> 01:07:37.760
I'm gonna get to all this stuff I wanna talk to you about.

1410
01:07:37.760 --> 01:07:39.980
So you're gonna definitely have to come back

1411
01:07:39.980 --> 01:07:41.320
and we can fill in the gaps.

1412
01:07:41.320 --> 01:07:44.030
But maybe we can shift gears a little bit here

1413
01:07:44.030 --> 01:07:47.113
and just talk about coaching in general.

1414
01:07:48.160 --> 01:07:49.340
This has been your career,

1415
01:07:49.340 --> 01:07:52.670
you're one of the great legendary coaches of all time.

1416
01:07:52.670 --> 01:07:54.720
So I'm interested in your thoughts about,

1417
01:07:55.860 --> 01:07:58.760
how you perceive the importance of coaching

1418
01:07:58.760 --> 01:08:02.680
and what are the distinguishing features of a great coach?

1419
01:08:02.680 --> 01:08:05.290
What makes a great coach great.

1420
01:08:05.290 --> 01:08:07.010
Well, I'll try to answer

1421
01:08:07.010 --> 01:08:08.860
the last part of it first.

1422
01:08:08.860 --> 01:08:12.740
I think what makes a great coach great

1423
01:08:12.740 --> 01:08:15.250
is his ability or her ability

1424
01:08:15.250 --> 01:08:19.290
to consistently get the athlete

1425
01:08:19.290 --> 01:08:21.320
to operate at peak efficiency.

1426
01:08:21.320 --> 01:08:25.160
To get the absolute best out of each individual

1427
01:08:25.160 --> 01:08:28.830
and to motivate them to greater heights.

1428
01:08:28.830 --> 01:08:33.740
To me, coaching is one of the greatest honors

1429
01:08:33.740 --> 01:08:36.630
you can ever have in your lifetime.

1430
01:08:36.630 --> 01:08:38.880
And so why do I feel that way?

1431
01:08:38.880 --> 01:08:41.240
Because a parent

1432
01:08:41.240 --> 01:08:45.390
is gonna take their most valuable possession, their child,

1433
01:08:45.390 --> 01:08:48.220
and they're gonna put them in your hands

1434
01:08:48.220 --> 01:08:51.450
and they're gonna allow you to influence them.

1435
01:08:51.450 --> 01:08:56.070
And so you have a huge responsibility.

1436
01:08:56.070 --> 01:08:58.640
I recruited a young man named Mark Boyd

1437
01:08:58.640 --> 01:09:01.700
from Stone Mountain in Georgia when I was at USC.

1438
01:09:01.700 --> 01:09:04.150
And after his mom signed the letter of intent

1439
01:09:04.150 --> 01:09:06.370
and she was walking me to the door,

1440
01:09:06.370 --> 01:09:08.097
she looked at me and she said,

1441
01:09:08.097 --> 01:09:10.970
"Coach Raveling, I don't want no foolishness outta you now."

1442
01:09:10.970 --> 01:09:12.240
And I knew what she meant.

1443
01:09:12.240 --> 01:09:16.640
Hey, I've raised this young man with a certain value system

1444
01:09:16.640 --> 01:09:19.380
and I expect you to expand on that,

1445
01:09:19.380 --> 01:09:21.610
I expect you to respect that.

1446
01:09:21.610 --> 01:09:26.590
And so to me, I feel sincerely

1447
01:09:26.590 --> 01:09:29.490
and this was a motivating factor with me.

1448
01:09:29.490 --> 01:09:32.180
If all a young man learned

1449
01:09:32.180 --> 01:09:35.010
during the four years he was with me

1450
01:09:35.010 --> 01:09:37.330
because in those days you didn't have one and done.

1451
01:09:37.330 --> 01:09:40.330
If all I taught him was how to shoot jump shots and rebound

1452
01:09:40.330 --> 01:09:42.660
and play defense and win basketball games,

1453
01:09:42.660 --> 01:09:44.570
hell could have gone anywhere and got that.

1454
01:09:44.570 --> 01:09:49.050
I want the four years with me to be a unique experience.

1455
01:09:49.050 --> 01:09:51.470
I want the things that he learned

1456
01:09:51.470 --> 01:09:55.280
to spill over from the basketball court into everyday life.

1457
01:09:55.280 --> 01:09:59.220
You have a responsibility as a coach to tell him

1458
01:09:59.220 --> 01:10:04.110
how the game athletics prepares him for the game of life

1459
01:10:04.110 --> 01:10:05.540
'cause at the end of the day,

1460
01:10:05.540 --> 01:10:08.730
the most important game to win is the game of life.

1461
01:10:08.730 --> 01:10:10.109
And so--

1462
01:10:10.109 --> 01:10:11.360
And what's your core philosophy

1463
01:10:11.360 --> 01:10:12.290
at the bottom of that?

1464
01:10:12.290 --> 01:10:13.123
Like what--

1465
01:10:13.123 --> 01:10:17.510
Well, I think it's a system that...

1466
01:10:17.510 --> 01:10:21.000
It's a secondary system of education.

1467
01:10:21.000 --> 01:10:23.890
You'd learn so much about life lessons

1468
01:10:23.890 --> 01:10:25.680
from participation in athletic.

1469
01:10:25.680 --> 01:10:27.100
You learn you don't win all the time,

1470
01:10:27.100 --> 01:10:28.240
you don't lose all the time.

1471
01:10:28.240 --> 01:10:29.830
You learn you have to sacrifice,

1472
01:10:29.830 --> 01:10:31.240
you have to be a team player,

1473
01:10:31.240 --> 01:10:32.970
you have to be teachable and coachable,

1474
01:10:32.970 --> 01:10:36.123
you have to play with whites and blacks and Hispanics.

1475
01:10:37.354 --> 01:10:39.300
You learn that you've gotta be a listener,

1476
01:10:39.300 --> 01:10:42.240
you gotta make quick decisions on the run.

1477
01:10:42.240 --> 01:10:47.240
There's so many things that you learn from athletics.

1478
01:10:47.330 --> 01:10:49.450
You learn leadership.

1479
01:10:49.450 --> 01:10:53.480
And so all these things are the early preparation

1480
01:10:54.540 --> 01:10:58.540
for your transformation from participating in sports

1481
01:10:58.540 --> 01:11:02.053
and you take those values with you into society.

1482
01:11:03.890 --> 01:11:07.870
But we have to get people to see the connection

1483
01:11:07.870 --> 01:11:10.900
between their participation

1484
01:11:10.900 --> 01:11:13.370
and its preparation for you in the future.

1485
01:11:13.370 --> 01:11:18.243
Very small segment of kids are gonna make it to the NBA

1486
01:11:19.530 --> 01:11:24.020
and probably less than 1% of the black kids in America

1487
01:11:24.020 --> 01:11:26.100
are ever gonna play in the NBA,

1488
01:11:26.100 --> 01:11:31.100
but their participation can be more important

1489
01:11:31.680 --> 01:11:36.680
and should more important than participating in the NBA.

1490
01:11:36.820 --> 01:11:40.260
That's what bothers me about this one and done.

1491
01:11:40.260 --> 01:11:44.440
One and done puts too much emphasis on material things

1492
01:11:44.440 --> 01:11:47.930
and money and not enough emphasis on education.

1493
01:11:47.930 --> 01:11:49.520
That's what I worry about it.

1494
01:11:49.520 --> 01:11:52.200
I don't deny that a young person

1495
01:11:52.200 --> 01:11:56.900
is physically talented enough to participate at that level,

1496
01:11:56.900 --> 01:11:58.270
but does he have the...

1497
01:11:58.270 --> 01:12:01.890
Does he have the educational fundamentals

1498
01:12:01.890 --> 01:12:02.830
that he's gonna need.

1499
01:12:02.830 --> 01:12:05.840
If you're making $100 million a year,

1500
01:12:05.840 --> 01:12:07.173
you can't even sit in the media

1501
01:12:07.173 --> 01:12:08.850
and ask an intelligent question

1502
01:12:08.850 --> 01:12:10.970
because you don't have the educational background.

1503
01:12:10.970 --> 01:12:12.380
Well, and if you don't have

1504
01:12:12.380 --> 01:12:15.140
a mature character, then you're more...

1505
01:12:15.140 --> 01:12:17.260
You're probably gonna make some bad decisions.

1506
01:12:17.260 --> 01:12:19.440
Absolutely.

1507
01:12:19.440 --> 01:12:22.930
And I used to do a talk called voices and choices

1508
01:12:22.930 --> 01:12:27.930
and the thesis is that the voices you listen to

1509
01:12:29.600 --> 01:12:33.510
are the ones that determine the choices that you make.

1510
01:12:33.510 --> 01:12:35.190
And so know at the end of the day

1511
01:12:35.190 --> 01:12:37.940
if you listen enough of the right voices,

1512
01:12:37.940 --> 01:12:39.350
you'll make the right choices.

1513
01:12:39.350 --> 01:12:42.360
If you listen enough to the wrong voices,

1514
01:12:42.360 --> 01:12:45.580
you're gonna make a bad choices and that.

1515
01:12:45.580 --> 01:12:50.100
And I think today, when you look at the compensation

1516
01:12:50.100 --> 01:12:51.970
for professional athletics,

1517
01:12:51.970 --> 01:12:55.183
education is more important today than it's ever was.

1518
01:12:56.221 --> 01:13:01.221
If you can couple education with a professional career

1519
01:13:01.400 --> 01:13:03.603
and the compensation that you have,

1520
01:13:04.460 --> 01:13:07.390
you can help change the world because you...

1521
01:13:07.390 --> 01:13:09.300
But you've got to be able

1522
01:13:09.300 --> 01:13:11.660
to put the physical with the mental.

1523
01:13:11.660 --> 01:13:15.140
See there's far too much emphasis on the physical now

1524
01:13:15.140 --> 01:13:16.977
and not enough emphasis on the mental.

1525
01:13:16.977 --> 01:13:18.920
Is that what you would characterize

1526
01:13:18.920 --> 01:13:21.450
as the most common mistake or misstep

1527
01:13:21.450 --> 01:13:23.150
that most coaches make.

1528
01:13:23.150 --> 01:13:24.250
Absolutely.

1529
01:13:26.074 --> 01:13:28.840
And it starts on the basketball court

1530
01:13:28.840 --> 01:13:31.860
because you hear of all the skill development today

1531
01:13:31.860 --> 01:13:36.400
in athletics, but it's so out of balance

1532
01:13:36.400 --> 01:13:39.780
because all the skill development does with the physical,

1533
01:13:39.780 --> 01:13:41.760
there's no one spending a whole lot of time

1534
01:13:41.760 --> 01:13:44.540
working with the athlete on the mental part of the game.

1535
01:13:44.540 --> 01:13:47.360
If you can combine the physical with the mental,

1536
01:13:47.360 --> 01:13:51.490
ooh, you got a heck of a thing going for you now.

1537
01:13:51.490 --> 01:13:52.760
One of the things that everybody says

1538
01:13:52.760 --> 01:13:55.870
about you is that you are this amazing recruiter.

1539
01:13:55.870 --> 01:13:58.500
You had an eye and an ability

1540
01:13:58.500 --> 01:14:01.580
to really spot talent like above and beyond.

1541
01:14:01.580 --> 01:14:05.990
So when you are in that capacity, what are you looking for?

1542
01:14:05.990 --> 01:14:09.300
Like, what is the defining characteristic

1543
01:14:09.300 --> 01:14:13.483
of the great athlete or the unpolished gem?

1544
01:14:14.570 --> 01:14:16.430
What is the difference between

1545
01:14:16.430 --> 01:14:18.870
somebody who in your eyes is good

1546
01:14:18.870 --> 01:14:20.200
or has the potential to be good

1547
01:14:20.200 --> 01:14:24.870
and that rare special athlete who could be fantastic?

1548
01:14:24.870 --> 01:14:27.020
I don't know that it's any one thing.

1549
01:14:27.020 --> 01:14:29.970
I would think at the end of the day

1550
01:14:31.170 --> 01:14:34.400
some of the things I try to look at an athlete

1551
01:14:35.911 --> 01:14:39.050
and not look at necessarily where he is now

1552
01:14:39.050 --> 01:14:40.600
or where she is now,

1553
01:14:40.600 --> 01:14:44.120
but where can they be 5 years or 10 years from now.

1554
01:14:44.120 --> 01:14:45.100
Are they coachable?

1555
01:14:45.100 --> 01:14:46.110
Are they teachable?

1556
01:14:46.110 --> 01:14:48.423
Do they have a passion for the game?

1557
01:14:50.710 --> 01:14:54.073
What type of skills do they have?

1558
01:14:56.040 --> 01:14:58.093
Have they reached their ceiling?

1559
01:14:59.010 --> 01:15:01.160
And one of the reasons

1560
01:15:01.160 --> 01:15:04.370
I think I achieved success at Washington state

1561
01:15:04.370 --> 01:15:07.870
was because I was never able to get to super star.

1562
01:15:07.870 --> 01:15:08.880
I think in recruiting

1563
01:15:08.880 --> 01:15:11.180
anybody can go out and watch Willie Major

1564
